window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2024-12-1", "source": "2024年12月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose the business school of your university is conducting a survey to collect students' opinions on the express delivery service industry in China. You are to write a response about its recent development and its impact on people's lives. You will have 30 minutes to write the essay. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "As a student invited to share my views in this survey, I am glad to comment on the express delivery industry in\nChina, which has grown at an astonishing pace in recent years.\nTo begin with, the recent development of this industry is remarkable. Delivery networks now reach even\nremote villages, and parcels that once took a week can be delivered overnight. Smart lockers and automated\nsorting centres, powered by big data, have made the whole process faster and more accurate than ever before.\nMore importantly, this development has reshaped our daily lives. With reliable next-day delivery, people can\nshop online for almost anything, from food to books, without leaving home. Such convenience saves us time\nand energy, and it has also created millions of jobs for delivery workers across the country.\nIn conclusion, China's express delivery industry has advanced rapidly and brought real benefits to ordinary\npeople. I firmly believe it will continue to make our lives even more convenient."}, "translation": {"prompt": "中国政府十分重视环境保护。近年来，中国在减少空气、水和土壤污染上取得了显著成效。为了不断改善人们的生活环境，中国采取了一系列有效措施，包括大力发展清洁能源，改善公共交通，推广共享单车，实施垃圾分类。通过这些措施，中国的城市和农村正在绿起来、美起来。中国还积极参与国际合作，为全球环境保护做出了重要贡献。", "sample": "The Chinese government attaches great importance to environmental protection. In recent years, China has made remarkable progress in reducing air, water and soil pollution. To continuously improve people's living environment, China has taken a series of effective measures, including developing clean energy, improving public transportation, promoting shared bikes and implementing garbage classification. Thanks to these measures, both urban and rural areas in China are becoming greener and more beautiful. China also actively participates in international cooperation, making important contributions to global environmental protection."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2024-12-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "When Toni Morrison died in 2019, the world lost one of its most influential authors. But Morrison was not an early success. Her first novel was not published until she was 39, and her last appeared when she was 84. And Morrison was not  __26__  in this regard. Numerous writers produce masterpieces well into their 70s and beyond. Such  __27__  accomplishments highlight an important point. Our capacity to speak, write and learn new vocabulary does not seem to  __28__  with age. Our eyesight may dim and our recall may weaken, but, by comparison, our ability to produce and to  __29__  language is well preserved into older adulthood.\nIndeed, the latest research that has emerged on language and aging shows that language mastery is a  __30__  that we begin as infants and continue on for the rest of our lives. Some aspects of our language abilities, such as our knowledge of word meanings,  __31__  improve during middle and late adulthood. One study, for example, found that adults over sixty had an average vocabulary size of over 21,000 words. The researchers also studied a  __32__  of college students and found that their average vocabulary contained  __33__  16,000 words. In another study, older adults, with an average age of 75,  __34__  better than participants in their youth or middle years on tasks that required them to determine the meaning of words. Thus, language seems to be a skill that, contrary to what many might  __35__  , does not weaken with age.", "matchIntro": "Why it's wrong to look at work-life balance as an achievement", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Few topics have been so endlessly analysed as work-life balance. The quest to attain this mysterious state has dominated discussion around careers for years—especially for working parents. The concept is often presented as something to achieve, or a goal to reach. And once you've reached it, congratulations: you've made it; you're a successful human being of the 21st century."}, {"label": "B", "text": "But the problem is that we often tell ourselves: \"I'm going to put in eight hours' worth of work, and then I'm going to put in eight hours' worth of 'me time', which will include my family, my hobbies, my workout, my everything,\" says Anat Lechner, clinical associate professor of management at New York University. \"I don't think it's such a simple formula.\""}, {"label": "C", "text": "And, according to new findings, it may not be. Some researchers are now encouraging us to stop thinking about work-life balance as an achievement that you either hit or don't. Instead, they suggest it may be more of a lifelong process—a continuous, never-ending exercise that requires self-awareness and timely adjustments. Researchers Ioana Lupu and Mayra Ruiz-Castro argue that work-life balance is \"a cycle, not an achievement\". In their 2020 study, the researchers interviewed nearly 80 employees at two London-based firms—an equal number of men and women between the ages of 30 and 50, all with at least one dependent child—who worked in middle or senior management roles."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Although it sounds like the respondents had a lot in common, here's what separated them: about 30% of the men and 50% of the women reported resisting working long hours. The other respondents, meanwhile, all worked long hours because they thought that's what successful professionals should do."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Lupu and Ruiz-Castro looked at those who rejected the long hours and they found that those workers actually had strikingly similar strategies for maintaining their work-life balance. They had a tendency to reflect and question assumptions in the name of self-awareness and regularly took steps to adjust the things standing in their way to work-life balance."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Lupu and Ruiz-Castro identified five steps that the respondents in the study who had better work-life balance used in their jobs. First, they paused and reconsidered beliefs such as \"I'm a professional, so I should work, work, work\", and asked themselves questions like, \"What's currently causing me stress?\" Second, after identifying the cause, they zeroed in on their resultant emotions. Did they feel angry, sad, energised? Third, they reprioritised, asking \"Is working long hours really worth cutting back on family time?\", for example. Fourth, they considered their alternatives: is there anything at work that could be changed to accommodate these new priorities? And finally, they implemented changes, like asking their supervisor for greater flexibility, or deciding not to take on every project that comes their way."}, {"label": "G", "text": "This five-step process is something anyone can adopt. Going through the steps, and constantly checking in with yourself, can help you shift and adapt your professional life to something that will better harmonise with your personal one. \"Awareness of your emotional state is essential in order to determine the changes you want to make in your work and in your life,\" says Lupu."}, {"label": "H", "text": "New York University's Lechner agrees that finding that balance is an ongoing pursuit. It's not simply about dividing up the hours in your day between work, the gym, kids and chores. If the underlying emotional sources of stress are still there, then the time you actually spend at home may not be enjoyable. \"We come home and even though physically we are there, mentally we still may be processing things that happened at work. We're not present,\" she says. What we call \"work-life balance\" is actually just a substitute to having a sense of fulfillment and contentment."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Of course, finding that balance probably shouldn't be something you have to do by yourself. Research by Erin Kelly, professor of work and organisation studies at MIT, shows companies and managers can play a key role in creating a better environment for workers. For her book Overload: How Good Jobs Went Bad and What to Do about It, she and co-author Phyllis Moen split more than 1,000 employees at a Fortune 500 company into two groups, one that worked under a management redesign and one that continued working within the existing management structure."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Under the management redesign, many steps were taken to ensure better work-life balance and prevent burnout(精疲力竭). Managers were regularly reminded to explicitly support their employees. Workers were allowed to make changes, like cancelling 9 am meetings. All of this was done in the name of increasing job satisfaction and giving workers greater flexibility, and to assure workers that it was something management was committed to. Unsurprisingly, Kelly and Moen found that employees in the redesign group reported less stress and less burnout. They were less likely to quit their jobs; indeed, over the next four years, they were 40% less likely to quit than those who kept working under the old policies."}, {"label": "K", "text": "\"Work-life balance is understood to be an individual's response, so people think 'it's up to me to manage the craziness of my work life'\", says Kelly. But organisations need to examine the demands they're placing on employees. \"The root problem is not how the two pieces of work and life come together. It's that we have unrealistic expectations of what we're asked to do on the work side.\" If your workplace isn't an environment where work-life balance is possible in the first place, any effort you attempt to make toward it on a personal level will be in vain."}, {"label": "L", "text": "That's a conversation that appears to be gathering pace. The new prevalence of remote and flexible working models will likely all play important roles in how we balance our professional and personal lives. And if it seems like finding that perfect balance remains difficult to achieve, the experts say that keeping some perspective can help. For millions of people, work is about being able to put food on the table. Talking about work-life balance \"is a very privileged conversation\", says Lechner. \"If we're reflecting, maybe we should also reflect on that.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "The weakening of the human connection to nature might be good for economic growth but is bad for people. A tipping point was reached in 2020 when human-made materials—such as steel, concrete and plastic—were found to weigh more than all life on Earth. Continuing to grow concrete forests rather than real ones is shortsighted. Simply being in the nearest wood has such health benefits that the Woodland Trust successfully lobbied for it to be prescribed by doctors.\nYet slipping from popular culture is the wonder and beauty of the natural world. For every three nature-related words in hit songs of the 1950s, researchers found, there was only slightly more than one 50 years later. It is not a moment too soon that teenagers will be able to take a natural history test, given that for decades children have been able to name more video game characters than wildlife species.\nPart of remedying this social disease would be for parliament to pass a \"right to grow\" law, allowing anyone to turn underused public spaces into vegetable and fruit gardens. The idea is for people to get back in touch with the soil—while producing food sustainably.\nVegetable planting has a respectable tradition. In April 1649, locals responded to high prices and food shortages by cultivating vegetables on common land in Southern England. The practice of throwing seed bombs to turn vacant plots of land green took off in 1970s New York, and has been revived(使复活) by green-thumbed(有园艺才能的) social media influencers who defy local US regulations in a war on ugly spots in cities.\nApart from the urgent task of providing more healthy nutrients to those who increasingly can't afford them, publicly accessible fruit and vegetable gardens connect what we eat to where it comes from—the means of production, if you will. They can make unlovely spaces lovely, and marry use and beauty as well as help promote a sense of community. Plants are also, of course, our first defence against species loss and climate change. Such planting is a small step for humanity—in the right direction.", "47": "The weakening of the human connection to nature might be good for economic growth but is bad for people. A tipping point was reached in 2020 when human-made materials—such as steel, concrete and plastic—were found to weigh more than all life on Earth. Continuing to grow concrete forests rather than real ones is shortsighted. Simply being in the nearest wood has such health benefits that the Woodland Trust successfully lobbied for it to be prescribed by doctors.\nYet slipping from popular culture is the wonder and beauty of the natural world. For every three nature-related words in hit songs of the 1950s, researchers found, there was only slightly more than one 50 years later. It is not a moment too soon that teenagers will be able to take a natural history test, given that for decades children have been able to name more video game characters than wildlife species.\nPart of remedying this social disease would be for parliament to pass a \"right to grow\" law, allowing anyone to turn underused public spaces into vegetable and fruit gardens. The idea is for people to get back in touch with the soil—while producing food sustainably.\nVegetable planting has a respectable tradition. In April 1649, locals responded to high prices and food shortages by cultivating vegetables on common land in Southern England. The practice of throwing seed bombs to turn vacant plots of land green took off in 1970s New York, and has been revived(使复活) by green-thumbed(有园艺才能的) social media influencers who defy local US regulations in a war on ugly spots in cities.\nApart from the urgent task of providing more healthy nutrients to those who increasingly can't afford them, publicly accessible fruit and vegetable gardens connect what we eat to where it comes from—the means of production, if you will. They can make unlovely spaces lovely, and marry use and beauty as well as help promote a sense of community. Plants are also, of course, our first defence against species loss and climate change. Such planting is a small step for humanity—in the right direction.", "48": "The weakening of the human connection to nature might be good for economic growth but is bad for people. A tipping point was reached in 2020 when human-made materials—such as steel, concrete and plastic—were found to weigh more than all life on Earth. Continuing to grow concrete forests rather than real ones is shortsighted. Simply being in the nearest wood has such health benefits that the Woodland Trust successfully lobbied for it to be prescribed by doctors.\nYet slipping from popular culture is the wonder and beauty of the natural world. For every three nature-related words in hit songs of the 1950s, researchers found, there was only slightly more than one 50 years later. It is not a moment too soon that teenagers will be able to take a natural history test, given that for decades children have been able to name more video game characters than wildlife species.\nPart of remedying this social disease would be for parliament to pass a \"right to grow\" law, allowing anyone to turn underused public spaces into vegetable and fruit gardens. The idea is for people to get back in touch with the soil—while producing food sustainably.\nVegetable planting has a respectable tradition. In April 1649, locals responded to high prices and food shortages by cultivating vegetables on common land in Southern England. The practice of throwing seed bombs to turn vacant plots of land green took off in 1970s New York, and has been revived(使复活) by green-thumbed(有园艺才能的) social media influencers who defy local US regulations in a war on ugly spots in cities.\nApart from the urgent task of providing more healthy nutrients to those who increasingly can't afford them, publicly accessible fruit and vegetable gardens connect what we eat to where it comes from—the means of production, if you will. They can make unlovely spaces lovely, and marry use and beauty as well as help promote a sense of community. Plants are also, of course, our first defence against species loss and climate change. Such planting is a small step for humanity—in the right direction.", "49": "The weakening of the human connection to nature might be good for economic growth but is bad for people. A tipping point was reached in 2020 when human-made materials—such as steel, concrete and plastic—were found to weigh more than all life on Earth. Continuing to grow concrete forests rather than real ones is shortsighted. Simply being in the nearest wood has such health benefits that the Woodland Trust successfully lobbied for it to be prescribed by doctors.\nYet slipping from popular culture is the wonder and beauty of the natural world. For every three nature-related words in hit songs of the 1950s, researchers found, there was only slightly more than one 50 years later. It is not a moment too soon that teenagers will be able to take a natural history test, given that for decades children have been able to name more video game characters than wildlife species.\nPart of remedying this social disease would be for parliament to pass a \"right to grow\" law, allowing anyone to turn underused public spaces into vegetable and fruit gardens. The idea is for people to get back in touch with the soil—while producing food sustainably.\nVegetable planting has a respectable tradition. In April 1649, locals responded to high prices and food shortages by cultivating vegetables on common land in Southern England. The practice of throwing seed bombs to turn vacant plots of land green took off in 1970s New York, and has been revived(使复活) by green-thumbed(有园艺才能的) social media influencers who defy local US regulations in a war on ugly spots in cities.\nApart from the urgent task of providing more healthy nutrients to those who increasingly can't afford them, publicly accessible fruit and vegetable gardens connect what we eat to where it comes from—the means of production, if you will. They can make unlovely spaces lovely, and marry use and beauty as well as help promote a sense of community. Plants are also, of course, our first defence against species loss and climate change. Such planting is a small step for humanity—in the right direction.", "50": "The weakening of the human connection to nature might be good for economic growth but is bad for people. A tipping point was reached in 2020 when human-made materials—such as steel, concrete and plastic—were found to weigh more than all life on Earth. Continuing to grow concrete forests rather than real ones is shortsighted. Simply being in the nearest wood has such health benefits that the Woodland Trust successfully lobbied for it to be prescribed by doctors.\nYet slipping from popular culture is the wonder and beauty of the natural world. For every three nature-related words in hit songs of the 1950s, researchers found, there was only slightly more than one 50 years later. It is not a moment too soon that teenagers will be able to take a natural history test, given that for decades children have been able to name more video game characters than wildlife species.\nPart of remedying this social disease would be for parliament to pass a \"right to grow\" law, allowing anyone to turn underused public spaces into vegetable and fruit gardens. The idea is for people to get back in touch with the soil—while producing food sustainably.\nVegetable planting has a respectable tradition. In April 1649, locals responded to high prices and food shortages by cultivating vegetables on common land in Southern England. The practice of throwing seed bombs to turn vacant plots of land green took off in 1970s New York, and has been revived(使复活) by green-thumbed(有园艺才能的) social media influencers who defy local US regulations in a war on ugly spots in cities.\nApart from the urgent task of providing more healthy nutrients to those who increasingly can't afford them, publicly accessible fruit and vegetable gardens connect what we eat to where it comes from—the means of production, if you will. They can make unlovely spaces lovely, and marry use and beauty as well as help promote a sense of community. Plants are also, of course, our first defence against species loss and climate change. Such planting is a small step for humanity—in the right direction.", "51": "Engineering in the U.S. has long been a male-dominated profession. Fifty years ago, it looked like that might change.\nIn 1970, the percentage of women majoring in engineering was less than 1%. In 1979, that number was 9%. Many hoped women would continue to enter the field at the same rate. But that's not what happened. Today, only 21% of engineering majors are women, a number largely unchanged since 2000.\nI am a historian who, along with my colleagues, surveyed 251 women engineers who graduated from college in the 1970s. These pioneers reflected on the challenges they faced—and had advice for women entering the field today.\nOne survey taker explained, \"The greatest challenge for me was continuing to believe in myself, when all the messages I was getting were that I would never be taken seriously or promoted or given raises at the same rate as men, who were clearly less qualified and not as smart as I was.\"\nA chemical engineer who worked in manufacturing agreed, \"You have to prove yourself just because you are female. And you have to work twice as hard!\"\nA civil engineer said, \"We are 'women engineers.' People don't refer to a man as a 'man engineer'—he's an engineer. We are constantly reminded that we don't truly belong.\" Another civil engineer stated, \"On many levels, you're never quite one of the groups.\"\nWomen also talked about family caregiving responsibilities. A retired vice president from a major chemical company stated, \"Young women engineers are on an equal footing until they have children, then they struggle to balance work and family—and compete with men who don't have the same household responsibilities.\"\nBut over the years things have changed a lot. Young women engineers are more accepted mostly because there are just more of them.\nMany women engineers hailed the benefits of their chosen career. A program manager in manufacturing stated that engineering is the best degree. A mechanical engineer said, \"It will give you the flexibility to do almost anything. It is also satisfying to see the effects of what you have done.\"", "52": "Engineering in the U.S. has long been a male-dominated profession. Fifty years ago, it looked like that might change.\nIn 1970, the percentage of women majoring in engineering was less than 1%. In 1979, that number was 9%. Many hoped women would continue to enter the field at the same rate. But that's not what happened. Today, only 21% of engineering majors are women, a number largely unchanged since 2000.\nI am a historian who, along with my colleagues, surveyed 251 women engineers who graduated from college in the 1970s. These pioneers reflected on the challenges they faced—and had advice for women entering the field today.\nOne survey taker explained, \"The greatest challenge for me was continuing to believe in myself, when all the messages I was getting were that I would never be taken seriously or promoted or given raises at the same rate as men, who were clearly less qualified and not as smart as I was.\"\nA chemical engineer who worked in manufacturing agreed, \"You have to prove yourself just because you are female. And you have to work twice as hard!\"\nA civil engineer said, \"We are 'women engineers.' People don't refer to a man as a 'man engineer'—he's an engineer. We are constantly reminded that we don't truly belong.\" Another civil engineer stated, \"On many levels, you're never quite one of the groups.\"\nWomen also talked about family caregiving responsibilities. A retired vice president from a major chemical company stated, \"Young women engineers are on an equal footing until they have children, then they struggle to balance work and family—and compete with men who don't have the same household responsibilities.\"\nBut over the years things have changed a lot. Young women engineers are more accepted mostly because there are just more of them.\nMany women engineers hailed the benefits of their chosen career. A program manager in manufacturing stated that engineering is the best degree. A mechanical engineer said, \"It will give you the flexibility to do almost anything. It is also satisfying to see the effects of what you have done.\"", "53": "Engineering in the U.S. has long been a male-dominated profession. Fifty years ago, it looked like that might change.\nIn 1970, the percentage of women majoring in engineering was less than 1%. In 1979, that number was 9%. Many hoped women would continue to enter the field at the same rate. But that's not what happened. Today, only 21% of engineering majors are women, a number largely unchanged since 2000.\nI am a historian who, along with my colleagues, surveyed 251 women engineers who graduated from college in the 1970s. These pioneers reflected on the challenges they faced—and had advice for women entering the field today.\nOne survey taker explained, \"The greatest challenge for me was continuing to believe in myself, when all the messages I was getting were that I would never be taken seriously or promoted or given raises at the same rate as men, who were clearly less qualified and not as smart as I was.\"\nA chemical engineer who worked in manufacturing agreed, \"You have to prove yourself just because you are female. And you have to work twice as hard!\"\nA civil engineer said, \"We are 'women engineers.' People don't refer to a man as a 'man engineer'—he's an engineer. We are constantly reminded that we don't truly belong.\" Another civil engineer stated, \"On many levels, you're never quite one of the groups.\"\nWomen also talked about family caregiving responsibilities. A retired vice president from a major chemical company stated, \"Young women engineers are on an equal footing until they have children, then they struggle to balance work and family—and compete with men who don't have the same household responsibilities.\"\nBut over the years things have changed a lot. Young women engineers are more accepted mostly because there are just more of them.\nMany women engineers hailed the benefits of their chosen career. A program manager in manufacturing stated that engineering is the best degree. A mechanical engineer said, \"It will give you the flexibility to do almost anything. It is also satisfying to see the effects of what you have done.\"", "54": "Engineering in the U.S. has long been a male-dominated profession. Fifty years ago, it looked like that might change.\nIn 1970, the percentage of women majoring in engineering was less than 1%. In 1979, that number was 9%. Many hoped women would continue to enter the field at the same rate. But that's not what happened. Today, only 21% of engineering majors are women, a number largely unchanged since 2000.\nI am a historian who, along with my colleagues, surveyed 251 women engineers who graduated from college in the 1970s. These pioneers reflected on the challenges they faced—and had advice for women entering the field today.\nOne survey taker explained, \"The greatest challenge for me was continuing to believe in myself, when all the messages I was getting were that I would never be taken seriously or promoted or given raises at the same rate as men, who were clearly less qualified and not as smart as I was.\"\nA chemical engineer who worked in manufacturing agreed, \"You have to prove yourself just because you are female. And you have to work twice as hard!\"\nA civil engineer said, \"We are 'women engineers.' People don't refer to a man as a 'man engineer'—he's an engineer. We are constantly reminded that we don't truly belong.\" Another civil engineer stated, \"On many levels, you're never quite one of the groups.\"\nWomen also talked about family caregiving responsibilities. A retired vice president from a major chemical company stated, \"Young women engineers are on an equal footing until they have children, then they struggle to balance work and family—and compete with men who don't have the same household responsibilities.\"\nBut over the years things have changed a lot. Young women engineers are more accepted mostly because there are just more of them.\nMany women engineers hailed the benefits of their chosen career. A program manager in manufacturing stated that engineering is the best degree. A mechanical engineer said, \"It will give you the flexibility to do almost anything. It is also satisfying to see the effects of what you have done.\"", "55": "Engineering in the U.S. has long been a male-dominated profession. Fifty years ago, it looked like that might change.\nIn 1970, the percentage of women majoring in engineering was less than 1%. In 1979, that number was 9%. Many hoped women would continue to enter the field at the same rate. But that's not what happened. Today, only 21% of engineering majors are women, a number largely unchanged since 2000.\nI am a historian who, along with my colleagues, surveyed 251 women engineers who graduated from college in the 1970s. These pioneers reflected on the challenges they faced—and had advice for women entering the field today.\nOne survey taker explained, \"The greatest challenge for me was continuing to believe in myself, when all the messages I was getting were that I would never be taken seriously or promoted or given raises at the same rate as men, who were clearly less qualified and not as smart as I was.\"\nA chemical engineer who worked in manufacturing agreed, \"You have to prove yourself just because you are female. And you have to work twice as hard!\"\nA civil engineer said, \"We are 'women engineers.' People don't refer to a man as a 'man engineer'—he's an engineer. We are constantly reminded that we don't truly belong.\" Another civil engineer stated, \"On many levels, you're never quite one of the groups.\"\nWomen also talked about family caregiving responsibilities. A retired vice president from a major chemical company stated, \"Young women engineers are on an equal footing until they have children, then they struggle to balance work and family—and compete with men who don't have the same household responsibilities.\"\nBut over the years things have changed a lot. Young women engineers are more accepted mostly because there are just more of them.\nMany women engineers hailed the benefits of their chosen career. A program manager in manufacturing stated that engineering is the best degree. A mechanical engineer said, \"It will give you the flexibility to do almost anything. It is also satisfying to see the effects of what you have done.\""}, "questions": [{"id": "2024-12-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What do we learn about Sherry Christian?", "options": ["A) She is making a special effort to be recognized.", "B) She has been living in Fayetteville for 25 years.", "C) She has been driving a school bus ever since 2009.", "D) She is the longest-serving bus driver in Fayetteville."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） She is the longest-serving bus driver in Fayetteville.\n【定位】As part of the event, the city is celebrating its longest-serving driver on the force.\n作为活动的一部分，该市正在表彰其在职时间最长的司机。\n【信号】最高级 longest-serving 强调。答案句的信号是最高级 longest-serving driver on the\nforce——『在职最久的司机』。最高级是新闻强调『独一份身份』的标志，一旦听到 the\nlongest / the most，往往就是出题点，要立刻锁定。\n【替换】the city is celebrating its longest-serving driver on the force ↔\nShe is the longest-serving bus driver in Fayetteville.\n同身份的换说法\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音里 The City of Fayetteville is making a special effort to recognize its bus drivers\n的主语是『城市』在努力表彰司机；选项 A 把它偷换成 She is making a special effort to\nbe recognized.——变成『她自己在努力争取被认可』。努力的人从城市换成了她本人，主\n语张冠李戴。\n借用原文：The City of Fayetteville is making a special effort to recognize its bus drivers\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音第 2 段说 Christian has been a driver for 25 years.——她当『司机』25 年；选项 B\n把这个 25 年的数字盗来，安到 living in Fayetteville for 25 years『在 Fayetteville 住了\n25 年』上。录音说的是开车 25 年、不是住了 25 年，听到 25 years 就选正中陷阱。\n借用原文：Christian has been a driver for 25 years.\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音第 3 段 Transit Driver Appreciation Day has been celebrated across the country\nas far back as 2009. 里的 2009，说的是『这个表彰日全国早在 2009 年就有了』，与她本\n人无关；选项 C 把 2009 盗来，说成 driving a school bus ever since 2009『她从 2009\n年起开校车』。年份对象被偷换，听到 2009 就选即中招。\n借用原文：Transit Driver Appreciation Day has been celebrated across the country as far back\nas 2009.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What did city officials of Fayetteville want bus drivers to be aware of?", "options": ["A) The importance of their service to the city.", "B) The number of riders they serve in the city.", "C) The new proclamation issued early this week.", "D) The significance of the event to take place March 18."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The importance of their service to the city.\n【定位】Officials said they want bus drivers to know how important they are to Fayetteville, serving\nalmost 1.6 million riders a year.\n官员们说，他们想让公交司机知道，他们对 Fayetteville 有多么重要，每年服务近 160 万名\n乘客。\n【信号】官方表态 said they want…to know。答案句的信号是官方直接表态 Officials said they\nwant bus drivers to know how important they are——『官员说想让司机知道他们有多\n重要』。said…want…to know 直接抛出他们要传达的内容，是答案句的明确路标。\n【替换】how important they are to Fayetteville ↔ The importance of their service to the city.\n形容词改名词\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音句尾 serving almost 1.6 million riders a year 确实念到了乘客数量，但它只是补充说\n明司机为何重要的背景；选项 B 把这个数字盗来当成官方想让司机知道的内容 The number\nof riders they serve in the city.。官方强调的是『重要性』，不是『乘客数量』这个数字本\n身，听到 riders 就选即中招。\n借用原文：serving almost 1.6 million riders a year\nC) 答非所问\n录音第 3 段 this week, the City of Fayetteville issued a proclamation 提到本周发了公\n告，选项 C 把它说成 The new proclamation issued early this week.。公告是事件背景，\n题目问的是『官方想让司机知道什么』，公告本身不是答案，属答非所问。\n借用原文：this week, the City of Fayetteville issued a proclamation for the day\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音 for the day to take place March 18th 念到了活动日期 3 月 18 日，选项 D 把它说成\nThe significance of the event to take place March 18.。日期只是活动安排的细节，官方\n想让司机知道的是『他们的重要性』而非『这个活动的意义』，听到 March 18 就选即被日\n期带偏。\n借用原文：for the day to take place March 18th\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 The Wilton School Board has proposed a 5.99% spending increase. If approved, it would be the\nlargest jump in at least eight budget cycles. The 5.99% request more than doubles the highest previous\nyear-over-year increase in the last eight years, which was 2.99% for the 2021-2022 school year. The\nbiggest driver of the $91.8 million proposed budget remains teacher salaries. These account for 64% of\nthe total request at $59 million. The $59 million is up from the $56.7 million budgeted for the current\nschool year.\nWilton学区委员会提议增加5.99%的支出。如果获得批准,这将是至少八个预算周期中最大的增幅。5.99%\n的增幅请求是过去八年中最高年度增幅的两倍多,而此前最高增幅是2021-2022学年的2.99%。这份9180\n万美元预算提案的最大驱动因素仍然是教师工资。教师工资占总请求金额的64%,为5900万美元。5900万\n美元高于本学年预算的5670万美元。\n2 There are some factors that could affect the budget moving forward. One of those is total enrollment.\nThe district will have a total enrollment of 3,742 students. Almost all of these students will be in\nkindergarten through high school, but 66 will be in prekindergarten and other programs. This represents\n47 fewer students than the current year. Fewer students mean less federal and state funding.\n有一些因素可能会影响未来的预算。其中之一是总入学人数。该学区将有3742名学生入学。几乎所有这\n些学生都将在幼儿园到高中阶段就读,但有66名学生将在学前班和其他项目中。这比本学年减少了47名学\n生。学生人数减少意味着联邦和州拨款减少。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the news report say the Wilton School Board has done?", "options": ["A) Budgeted $56.7 million for the coming school year.", "B) Requested to raise teachers' salaries by 3 percent.", "C) Proposed a spending increase by 5.99 percent.", "D) Kept raising funds for at least 8 budget cycles."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Proposed a spending increase by 5.99 percent.\n【定位】The Wilton School Board has proposed a 5.99% spending increase.\n威尔顿学区校董会提出了一项 5.99% 的支出增长。\n【信号】开篇首句直陈主体动作。新闻把核心事实放在开篇，第一句 The Wilton School Board has\nproposed a 5.99% spending increase. 直接交代了校董会做的事，主体+动作+数字俱\n全，正是答案句。\n【替换】has proposed a 5.99% spending increase ↔ Proposed a spending increase by 5.99 percent.\n原句几乎平移到选项\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nBudgeted $56.7 million for the coming school year. 借用了录音真实念到的数字 56.7\nmillion，可录音说的是 up from the $56.7 million budgeted for the current school\nyear——这是『本学年』已编的旧预算、是被比较的基数，并非为『下一学年』新编的，听\n到 56.7 million 就选会掉坑。\n借用原文：up from the $56.7 million budgeted for the current school year\nB) 原词陷阱\nRequested to raise teachers' salaries by 3 percent. 看似与教师薪资相关，但录音只说薪\n资占比 64% of the total request 与金额升至 59 million，从未说按 3% 加薪；3 percent\n与录音里的 2.99% 近似，是用近似数字加上『加薪』这个貌似合理的动作拼出来的干扰。\n借用原文：2.99% for the 2021-2022 school year\nD) 原词陷阱\nKept raising funds for at least 8 budget cycles. 抓住了录音里的 at least eight budget\ncycles，但录音原意是『这是至少八个预算周期以来最大的一次涨幅』，eight budget\ncycles 是个时间比较范围，并非校董会『持续筹款八个周期』这个动作，属于把时间状语硬\n读成动作。\n借用原文：the largest jump in at least eight budget cycles", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What will a decline in the total enrollment lead to?", "options": ["A) Decrease in salaries for school administrators.", "B) Reduction in federal and state funding.", "C) Firing of less qualified faculty members.", "D) Closing down of some less competitive schools."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Reduction in federal and state funding.\n【定位】This represents 47 fewer students than the current year. Fewer students mean less federal and\nstate funding.\n这意味着比本学年少了 47 名学生。学生减少意味着联邦和州的拨款减少。\n【信号】因果词 mean 点出后果。录音用 Fewer students mean less federal and state funding\n中的 mean 直接把『学生减少』和『拨款减少』连起来，mean 后面那一截就是题目要的后\n果，是标准的因果信号句。\n【替换】less federal and state funding ↔ Reduction in federal and state funding.\nless 换成名词 Reduction\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nDecrease in salaries for school administrators. 里 salaries 一词录音确实出现过（教师薪\n资升至 59 million），但录音只说薪资是上涨的、且针对的是教师而非行政人员，更没说招\n生下降会让薪资下降，是把听过的 salaries 拿来嫁接的无中生有后果。\n借用原文：teacher salaries. These account for 64% of the total request at $59 million\nC) 未提及\nFiring of less qualified faculty members. 听上去『钱少了就裁人』很合逻辑，但录音对学\n生减少给出的唯一后果是拨款减少，从未提到裁教师，这是用常识脑补、原文未提。\nD) 未提及\nClosing down of some less competitive schools. 同样是凭常识想象的后果，录音谈的是\n同一个学区内部的预算与拨款，根本没提到关停任何学校，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do we learn about thundersnows from the report?", "options": ["A) They often take place in the Scottish highlands.", "B) They usually happen in the middle of the night.", "C) Their damage can be as serious as that of bombs.", "D) Their loud claps can be confused with explosions."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Their loud claps can be confused with explosions.\n【定位】The phenomenon happens when thunder and lightning mix with a heavy snowstorm—\nsometimes causing loud claps that can be confused with explosions.\n这一现象发生在雷电与强暴风雪交织之时——有时会造成响亮的轰鸣声，让人误以为是\n爆炸。\n【信号】定义句\"The phenomenon happens when…\"。信号是这句对现象下定义的结构 The\nphenomenon happens when（这一现象发生在……之时），新闻在介绍一个陌生概念后，\n紧跟的这种\"定义/解释句\"就是该现象特征的承载句，听到\"The phenomenon…\"就锁定它\n后面的内容。\n【替换】loud claps that can be confused with explosions ↔\nTheir loud claps can be confused with explosions.\n原句换主语几乎平移\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音开头只说这次事件发生在苏格兰这一具体地点，并没说雷雪\"往往发生在苏格兰高地\"。\nA 借\"Scotland/highlands\"这个地名营造熟悉感，把\"这一次在苏格兰\"偷换成\"通常在高\n地\"，是个频率/范围被夸大的陷阱。\n借用原文：A rare thundersnow event has taken place in Scotland.\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音里 woken up in the middle of the night 说的是\"某个人这一次在半夜被吵醒\"，并\n非\"雷雪通常发生在半夜\"。B 把这个时间状语原词搬过来，又加上\"usually\"夸成惯例，听\n到\"middle of the night\"就选 B 正中陷阱。\n借用原文：Another person was woken up in the middle of the night\nC) 偷换概念\n录音说的是雷雪的\"巨响\"可被误当爆炸（声音层面），并没说它的\"破坏力\"和炸弹一样大。\nC 借用\"bombs\"这个原词，把\"声音像炸弹\"偷换成\"破坏力像炸弹\"，听到 bombs 就选会掉\n进去。\n借用原文：loud claps that can be confused with explosions", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What did the police do when they received the local residents' phone calls?", "options": ["A) They sent out a message to calm them down.", "B) They promised to stop the construction work.", "C) They told them the military exercise was over.", "D) They set off to inspect the site of the plane crash."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They sent out a message to calm them down.\n【定位】The police were flooded with many calls from the public. And they sent out a message asking\npeople to remain calm.\n警方接到了公众打来的大量电话。随后他们发出一条消息，请大家保持冷静。\n【信号】主语 the police 紧跟的动作句。信号是以警方为主语、紧接\"接到大量电话\"之后的那个动\n作 they sent out a message asking people to remain calm。题目问\"接到电话后做了什\n么\"，录音里\"接到电话\"这句的下一句就是答案落点，顺着叙事次序就能接住。\n【替换】sent out a message asking people to remain calm ↔ They sent out a message to calm them down.\n\"请人保持冷静\"=\"使其平静\"\n【排除】B) 未提及\n录音只提到有人\"以为是施工开工\"，从没说警方\"承诺停止施工\"。B 借\"construction\"这个\n画面把不存在的承诺安到警方头上，属凭空捏造的动作。\n借用原文：what he thought was early construction work starting\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音是警方\"向大家保证这只是雷电、不是军事演习\"，并非\"告诉大家军演结束了\"。C 偷\n来\"military exercise\"原词，把\"不是军演\"改成\"军演已结束\"——录音根本没有军演，何来\n结束，听到 military exercise 就选 C 正中陷阱。\n借用原文：it was just thunder and lightning rather than some military exercise\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里\"plane crash\"只是某人形容声音\"像飞机失事\"的比喻，根本没有真飞机失事，警方更\n没\"前往勘查失事现场\"。D 把这个比喻里的原词搬来，编出一个不存在的警方动作，听到\nplane crash 就选会掉坑。\n借用原文：described it as a loud boom, similar to a plane crash", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What were people advised to do when traveling outside?", "options": ["A) Keep warm.", "B) Take caution.", "C) Avoid driving.", "D) Use rail service."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Take caution.\n【定位】The weather was the coldest it had been for the season, so people were warned to be careful\nwhen traveling outside.\n天气是本季最冷的，因此人们被提醒外出时要小心。\n【信号】提醒信号\"were warned to be careful\"。信号是 people were warned to be careful\nwhen traveling outside 里的\"were warned\"（被提醒）这一提醒标志词，它后面紧跟的\nbe careful 就是\"被建议做什么\"的直接答案，听到 warned/advised 就锁后面的动作。\n【替换】people were warned to be careful ↔ Take caution\n\"be careful\"=\"take caution\"\n【排除】A) 过度推断\n录音虽说\"本季最冷\"，但提醒的内容是\"小心\"，并没说让大家\"保暖\"。A 借\"最冷天气\"的语\n境顺势推出\"keep warm\"，是录音没明说的过度延伸，听到 coldest 就联想到保暖会被带\n偏。\n借用原文：The weather was the coldest it had been for the season\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音说\"结冰湿滑路面会给司机带来困难\"，是提醒司机小心，并没说\"别开车\"。C\n借\"drivers\"原词把\"开车要当心\"偷换成\"避免开车\"，比录音说得更绝对，听到 drivers 就选\nC 会掉坑。\n借用原文：dangerous conditions such as icy roads or wet ground could cause difficulty for drivers\nD) 与原文相反\n录音说\"铁路服务也宣布列车可能因大雪受扰\"，是说火车也受影响、不可靠，绝非建议大\n家\"改坐火车\"。D 偷来\"rail service\"原词，把\"火车受影响\"反着说成\"用火车出行\"，听到 rail\nservice 就选 D 正中反向陷阱。\n借用原文：The rail service also announced potential disruptions to trains due to the deep snow.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the woman say she was actually wondering about?", "options": ["A) Why the man is so anxious to buy a new phone.", "B) How the man could possibly afford a new phone.", "C) How the man got into the habit of being wasteful.", "D) Why the man is so fond of using his credit card."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） How the man could possibly afford a new phone.\n【定位】No, actually, I was wondering how you could possibly afford a new phone.\n不，其实我是在想，你到底怎么买得起一部新手机。\n【信号】actually 纠偏后的真意。女方先用 No 否定男方的猜测，再用 actually 引出自己真正在想\n的事——\"actually\"后面才是她的本意，这正是答案信号：纠正/转折之后的内容才是落点。\n【替换】how you could possibly afford a new phone ↔ How the man could possibly afford a new phone.\n几乎原句搬运，仅换人称\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确有男方 I'm going to the city centre to buy a new phone today. 这种\"今天就去\n买\"的急切感，A 借此造\"为何这么急\"的诱惑；但女方明说在想的是\"怎么买得起\"，不是\"为\n何急\"。\n借用原文：I'm going to the city centre to buy a new phone today.\nC) 原词陷阱\n女方确实说了 getting another phone so soon is wasteful，C 盗这个 wasteful 来骗；但\n这是她在 But now that you mention it 之后补的评价，题干问的是她\"actually was\nwondering\"的那件事，那件事是\"如何负担\"，不是\"如何变浪费\"。\n借用原文：getting another phone so soon is wasteful\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音后文男方说 I just got a credit card，D 借\"信用卡\"造\"为何爱用卡\"的问题；但这是男\n方的话、且在女方发问之后，女方\"在想\"的内容里根本没有它。\n借用原文：I just got a credit card", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "Why is the man a little embarrassed by the phone he's using now?", "options": ["A) It is not in fashion any more.", "B) It goes out of order frequently.", "C) It is not as fancy as his colleagues'.", "D) It lacks functions office work requires."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It is not as fancy as his colleagues'.\n【定位】Maybe you're right, but the thing is, everyone at the office has a nice, expensive phone, and\nI'm a little embarrassed by mine.\n也许你说得对，但问题是，办公室里每个人都有一部又好又贵的手机，我对自己这部有点难\n为情。\n【信号】the thing is 引出真正缘由。男方用 but the thing is 转入正题，紧接着给出难为情的原因\n——\"大家都有又好又贵的手机\"。\"the thing is\"是\"我接下来说的才是要点\"的提示，正是答\n案信号。\n【替换】everyone at the office has a nice, expensive phone ↔ It is not as fancy as his colleagues'.\n正话反说＋同义改写\nI'm a little embarrassed by mine ↔ It is not as fancy as his colleagues'.\n难为情对应的正是\"比不上\"\n【排除】A) 未提及\n男方只说自己手机比不上同事、并非\"不再时髦\"。in fashion 这层意思录音从未出现，是\n凭\"旧手机\"常识硬加的。\nB) 未提及\n男方难为情是因为手机不够气派，不是因为它老出故障。\"goes out of order\"录音里完全没\n有对应。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 everyone at the office 出现\"office\"，D 用 functions office work requires\n把\"office\"接到\"工作功能缺失\"上；但男方难为情是\"不如别人气派\"，从没说手机缺工作所需\n功能。\n借用原文：everyone at the office has a nice, expensive phone", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the man say about the credit card he just got?", "options": ["A) It enables him to buy a new phone at a more favorable price.", "B) It is a special offer to recent college graduates working in offices.", "C) It is available to office workers who have a good enough credit score.", "D) It allows him to borrow money without paying interest for six months."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It allows him to borrow money without paying interest for six months.\n【定位】Maybe you're right, but the credit card has a very good special offer, where I don't pay any\ninterest for six months.\n也许你说得对，但这张信用卡有个很不错的特别优惠，六个月内我不用付任何利息。\n【信号】数字/期限信息点。男方介绍卡时给出 I don't pay any interest for six months——\"不付\n利息\"加\"六个月\"是一处明确的数字/条件信息，正是该题的答案信号，听到数字就该锁定。\n【替换】I don't pay any interest for six months ↔\nIt allows him to borrow money without paying interest for six months.\n同义改写＋补出隐含的\"借钱\"\n【排除】A) 未提及\n卡的好处是\"免息\"而非\"以更优价格买手机\"。a more favorable price 录音里没有对应，免\n息不等于降价。\nB) 张冠李戴\n录音里女方说 you are a recent graduate，B 借此编出\"专给应届办公族的特别优惠\"；但\n这是女方议论男方身份的话，与卡的优惠对象无关，男方介绍卡时根本没提面向谁。\n借用原文：you are a recent graduate\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音反复出现 credit card 的 credit，C 借此造 a good enough credit score 的门槛；但\n男方说的是\"免息\"，从没提任何信用分要求，credit score 是凭 credit 一词联想出来的。\n借用原文：the credit card has a very good special offer", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What do we learn about one of the woman's colleagues?", "options": ["A) She borrowed money to pay her debt.", "B) She developed a habit of overspending.", "C) She enjoys buying a lot of fancy things.", "D) She regrets ignoring the woman's advice."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） She developed a habit of overspending.\n【定位】One of my colleagues bought a lot of things on credit during her first year of work, and it\nbecame a bad habit, and she accumulated a lot of debt.\n我的一个同事在工作第一年用信用卡买了一大堆东西，后来变成了一个坏习惯，她还欠下了\n一大笔债。\n【信号】举例＋\"became a bad habit\"落点。女方用 One of my colleagues 引出例子，关键落在\nit became a bad habit——\"变成一个坏习惯\"是这句话的结论，正是答案信号：举例句里\n那个\"演变成…\"的结果才是要点。\n【替换】bought a lot of things on credit ... it became a bad habit ↔\nShe developed a habit of overspending.\n\"乱买成习惯\"概括为 overspending habit\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 she accumulated a lot of debt 出现 debt，A 借此编出\"借钱去还债\"；但原文只\n说她\"欠了债\"，从没说她\"再借钱来还\"，多绕了一步。\n借用原文：she accumulated a lot of debt\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音说她 bought a lot of things on credit，C 改成\"enjoys buying a lot of fancy\nthings\"；\"fancy（华而不实/高档）\"与\"enjoys（乐在其中）\"录音都没有，原文重点是\"成\n了坏习惯\"而非\"她爱买好东西\"。\n借用原文：bought a lot of things on credit\nD) 张冠李戴\n后文男方说 I'm sure she regrets it，D 借此说\"她后悔没听劝\"；但这是男方的推测、且\"没\n听 the woman 的劝\"录音根本没有，属把男方的话安到同事头上。\n借用原文：I'm sure she regrets it", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does the tiny home movement mainly advocate?", "options": ["A) Blog writing and natural resources saving.", "B) Joint efforts to improve the local community.", "C) Audience participation in The Morning Show.", "D) Home ownership and environmental protection."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题） Home ownership and environmental protection.\n【定位】Well, it's mainly about increasing home ownership and protecting the environment.\n嗯，它主要就是关于提高住房拥有率和保护环境。\n【信号】被问者应答 + mainly 点题。前一段主持人直接发问\"can you tell our audience what it's\nabout\"，紧接着这段是被问者 Bob 的正面应答，且自带点题词 it's mainly about——对话\n题里\"提问后被问者的第一句正面回答\"加上 mainly 这种总括词，正是答案句的双重信号。\n【替换】increasing home ownership ↔ Home ownership\n动名词短语压缩成名词\nprotecting the environment ↔ environmental protection\n动词短语名词化\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音开头确实说嘉宾是 a popular blog writer，后文也提到省 fewer resources，本项\nBlog writing and natural resources saving 把这两个零散原词拼到一起冒充主旨，但\"写\n博客\"只是嘉宾身份、不是这个运动主张的内容，答非所问。\n借用原文：a popular blog writer\nB) 未提及\nJoint efforts to improve the local community 说\"合力改善本地社区\"，但录音通篇只讲\n购房与环保，从未提到\"社区\"或\"共同努力\"，属凭空添加。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音里 The Morning Show 只是这档节目的名字，本项 Audience participation in The\nMorning Show 借这个耳熟的专有名词，硬说运动主张是\"观众参与节目\"，与运动内容毫不\n相干。\n借用原文：The Morning Show", "points": ["听力·篇章", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the woman say about houses as small as 20 square meters?", "options": ["A) Not a realistic size for families.", "B) Not welcomed by most Americans.", "C) Only fit for families of four or less.", "D) Only suitable for renting to the poor."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Not a realistic size for families.\n【定位】But I've seen your blog, and you write about houses that are as small as 20 square meters.\nThat's not a realistic size for families.\n但是我看过你的博客，你写的是小到只有 20 平方米的房子。对家庭来说那可不是一个现实\n的尺寸。\n【信号】转折 But + 直接否定评价。这句以 But 转折开头，女方先承认目标好、随即话锋一转给出\n否定评价 That's not a realistic size for families——转折后那句才是她的真实立场，正是\n答案句信号。题干问\"the woman 说了什么\"，定位到女方说话的这一段即可。\n【替换】That's not a realistic size for families ↔ Not a realistic size for families\n去掉主系结构后近乎原意\n【排除】B) 未提及\nNot welcomed by most Americans 说\"不受多数美国人欢迎\"，但女方只说尺寸\"不现\n实\"，从没提\"美国人\"或\"受不受欢迎\"，是凭空替换成另一种否定理由。\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音后面男方才提到 many families of four are happy living（四口之家也住得开），本项\nOnly fit for families of four or less 盗用\"four\"这个数字，还用 Only 把范围限死成\"仅四\n口以下\"。这既张冠李戴（那是男方后说的、且态度相反），又把话说绝了。\n借用原文：many families of four are happy living in houses that are under 30 square meters\nD) 未提及\nOnly suitable for renting to the poor 说\"只适合租给穷人\"，录音里女方对小房子只评价\n了\"尺寸不现实\"，从未谈到\"出租\"或\"穷人\"，纯属添加。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the man think about the dream of owning a 150-square-meter house?", "options": ["A) It should be changed.", "B) It isn't easy to realize.", "C) It will come true sooner or later.", "D) It doesn't appeal to average families."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） It should be changed.\n【定位】Yes, but I think that dream needs to change, considering the cost of housing.\n是的，但我认为考虑到住房成本，那个梦想需要改变。\n【信号】but + I think + needs to change 表态。前一段女方说大房子\"是人们梦想拥有的\"，男方\n先 Yes 让步、随即用 but I think that dream needs to change 转折表态。but + I think\n是态度信号，needs to change（需要改变）直接亮明他的立场，正是答案句。\n【替换】that dream needs to change ↔ It should be changed\n主动 needs to 改成被动 should be\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\n录音男方提到 considering the cost of housing（考虑住房成本），本项 It isn't easy to\nrealize 借\"成本高\"联想成\"梦想不易实现\"，但男方的原话是\"梦想该改\"，不是\"难实现\"，偷\n换了概念。\n借用原文：considering the cost of housing\nC) 与原文相反\nIt will come true sooner or later 说\"迟早会实现\"，方向与男方\"该改变\"完全相反。男方否\n定的是这个大房梦本身，绝不会说它迟早成真。\n借用原文：that dream needs to change\nD) 未提及\nIt doesn't appeal to average families 说\"对普通家庭没吸引力\"，但男方对这个 150 平米\n梦想只说了\"该改\"，从没说\"对谁没吸引力\"，是另造一个评价。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the man say about tiny homes?", "options": ["A) They help to lower housing prices.", "B) They help to save up for larger houses.", "C) They are a good choice for many people.", "D) They are vital to reducing waste worldwide."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （态度观点题） They are a good choice for many people.\n【定位】So, tiny homes might not be for everyone, but they're a good option for many.\n所以呢，微型住宅可能不适合所有人，但对很多人来说是个好选择。\n【信号】So 收束 + but 后的总评。这句以 So 收束前面关于省钱的讨论，男方先让步\"未必适合所有\n人\"、再用 but 给出总评 they're a good option for many。So 后的总结句加 but 后的真\n态度，是这道总评题的双重信号。\n【替换】they're a good option for many ↔ They are a good choice for many people\noption=choice、many=many people\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n录音谈到小房子便宜、能省钱 a tiny home costs just \\$50,000，本项 They help to\nlower housing prices 借\"价格\"话题，偷换成\"帮助拉低房价\"。男方说的是\"个人买得起、\n省钱\"，不是\"压低市场房价\"，概念被偷换。\n借用原文：Many people who can only afford to rent a large home are able to buy a tiny home\nB) 与原文相反\n录音说省下的钱让人能\"买下小房子\"，本项 They help to save up for larger houses 把方\n向拧反成\"攒钱去买更大的房子\"。这与男方\"买小房子、改掉大房梦\"的主张正相反。\n借用原文：are able to buy a tiny home\nD) 过度推断\n录音只在结尾说小房子\"用更少土地与资源\" they use less land and fewer resources，本\n项 They are vital to reducing waste worldwide 用 vital（至关重要）、worldwide（全世\n界）夸大成\"对全球减废至关重要\"，程度和范围都远超原话。\n借用原文：they use less land and fewer resources to build them", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What do children seem to have a natural desire to do?", "options": ["A) Play outdoors and enjoy themselves.", "B) Beat challenges and take risks.", "C) Take care of their well-being.", "D) Grow up strong and healthy."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Beat challenges and take risks.\n【定位】To begin with, children seem to have a natural desire to overcome challenges and take risks.\n首先，孩子们似乎天生就有克服挑战、承担风险的渴望。\n【信号】序数词 To begin with 领出第一个要点。To begin with 是篇章列举的第一个序数词，它后\n面紧跟的就是本段第一个论点句，也是题干里 natural desire 原话的落点，听到序数词起头\n就要立刻锁定其后这句。\n【替换】overcome challenges and take risks ↔ Beat challenges and take risks\novercome 同义替换为 Beat\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文开头确有 run, jump, stretch and play，又多处讲 pleasure，选项 Play outdoors\nand enjoy themselves. 拼出 play 与 enjoy 听着耳熟，但这些不是孩子天生渴望要做的那\n件事，与题干 natural desire 错位。\n借用原文：run, jump, stretch and play\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文有 improve emotional well-being，选项 Take care of their well-being. 借了 well-\nbeing 这个原词，但提升幸福感是运动带来的好处，不是孩子天生的渴望，把别处的内容安\n到本题上。\n借用原文：improve emotional well-being\nD) 未提及\nGrow up strong and healthy. 是人人认同的常识，听着最顺，但原文说孩子天生渴望的是\n克服挑战、承担风险，并未说他们天生渴望健康成长，凭印象选它就中了陷阱。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "According to the American Psychological Association, what does regular physical activity do to children?", "options": ["A) Enable them to develop their motor skills.", "B) Help them to conceal their frustration.", "C) Reduce their stress and depression.", "D) Prevent them from feeling lonely."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Reduce their stress and depression.\n【定位】Thirdly, according to the American Psychological Association, regular physical activity (and\nespecially outdoor activity) reduces children's stress and depression, and improves their\nability to focus and learn.\n第三，根据美国心理学会的说法，规律的身体活动（尤其是户外活动）能减轻孩子的压力和\n抑郁，并提升他们专注和学习的能力。\n【信号】出处词 according to 与题干同源对接。原文 according to the American Psychological\nAssociation 与题干出处一字不差，听到这个机构名就知道答案句到了，其后 reduces\nchildren's stress and depression 即为落点。\n【替换】reduces children's stress and depression ↔ Reduce their stress and depression\n近乎同表述、仅人称代词改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文 develop their motor skills 确有这个搭配，选项 Enable them to develop their\nmotor skills. 原词照搬，听着最像，但这是第一段讲运动总体好处时说的，不属于美国心理\n学会出处句，被题干的出处限定排除。\n借用原文：develop their motor skills\nB) 偷换概念\n原文出现 persist through frustration 与 positive outlet for frustration，选项 Help\nthem to conceal their frustration. 借 frustration 一词，却把原文的疏导挫折偷换成隐藏\n挫折，方向相反。\n借用原文：positive outlet for frustration\nD) 未提及\nPrevent them from feeling lonely. 听起来合理，但原文讲的是减压、抗抑郁、助专注，从\n未提到防止孤独，属于无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does the passage say about cultivating the habit of physical activity?", "options": ["A) It begins early.", "B) It proves demanding.", "C) It calls for lifelong efforts.", "D) It lies in nature experiences."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It begins early.\n【定位】Finally, we know that physical activity is important for our physical and mental health, and\ncultivating the habit of physical activity starts early.\n最后，我们知道身体活动对我们的身心健康很重要，而养成身体活动的习惯要从早开始。\n【信号】序数词 Finally 领出末点、starts early 是定性词。Finally 标出最后一个要点，紧接着\ncultivating the habit of physical activity starts early 直接对养成习惯下定性，starts\nearly 就是答案的核心信息。\n【替换】cultivating the habit of physical activity starts early ↔ It begins early\nstarts 同义替换为 begins、习惯被代词 It 概括\n【排除】B) 未提及\nIt proves demanding. 说养成习惯很费劲，但原文只说它 starts early，并未评价它吃力，\n属于无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文确有 a healthy coping skill throughout life 和 a lifelong love of physical activity，\n选项 It calls for lifelong efforts. 借 lifelong 这个原词，但原文是说会终身热爱、终身受\n用，绝不是说养成习惯需要终身努力，把听到的 lifelong 安错了对象，也与本题定性句\nstarts early 矛盾。\n借用原文：a lifelong love of physical activity\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文结尾确有 gardening or nature experiences，选项 It lies in nature experiences. 搬\n了 nature experiences 这个原词，但那是在举可替代的活动选项，不是在说养成习惯这件\n事的特点，答非所问。\n借用原文：gardening or nature experiences", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "When are job seekers willing to explore jobs with lower salaries, according to a new study?", "options": ["A) When companies share their information online.", "B) When their job search criteria are met fully.", "C) When companies embrace greater diversity", "D) When they find job postings visually attractive."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） When companies embrace greater diversity\n【定位】A new study finds that job seekers are even willing to explore jobs with lower salaries when\ncompanies are more diverse.\n一项新研究发现，当企业更具多样性时，求职者甚至愿意去考虑薪资更低的工作。\n【信号】when 条件状语点出触发条件。这句用 when companies are more diverse 这个条件状\n语，直接把『愿接受低薪』的触发条件说出来，正是题干 When 所问。开篇第一句即点\n题，是篇章独白的典型落点。\n【替换】when companies are more diverse ↔ When companies embrace greater diversity\n形容词转动词的同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音后文确有 sharing information about diversity 和提到在线平台，A 项 share their\ninformation online 盗用了 information、online 这些原词，但它说的是公司在网上分享\n信息，并非求职者接受低薪的触发条件，听到原词就选会落入陷阱。\n借用原文：sharing information about diversity\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音提到平台按 their job search criteria 推送邮件，B 项 When their job search criteria\nare met fully 借用了 job search criteria 这个原词，但录音从没说『求职标准被完全满足』\n才接受低薪，这是用原词拼出的无中生有条件。\n借用原文：their job search criteria\nD) 偷换概念\n录音说多样性让 job postings more attractive，D 项 When they find job postings\nvisually attractive. 盗用了 job postings、attractive，却加了 visually（视觉上）这层录音\n没有的意思，把『内容上更有吸引力』偷换成『看起来好看』。\n借用原文：more attractive to job seekers", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What did participants see in the baseline condition of the study?", "options": ["A) Emails in the normal format.", "B) Major companies in one location.", "C) All companies in the same sector.", "D) Various aspects of a company's workforce."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Emails in the normal format.\n【定位】In the baseline condition, participants saw emails in the normal format, with no information\non diversity; in the diversity condition, participants saw a diversity score for each listing.\n在基线组，参与者看到的是普通格式的邮件，不含任何多样性信息；在多样性组，参与者则\n看到每条招聘的多样性评分。\n【信号】限定词 baseline condition 直接领出答案。录音用 In the baseline condition,\nparticipants saw emails in the normal format 直接点名基线组及其所见，与题干限定词\n完全对上，是顺听即可锁定的答案句；分号后是另一组，须切开别混。\n【替换】participants saw emails in the normal format ↔ Emails in the normal format.\n近乎原句的概括\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音末尾提到 others in the same sector and location，B 项 Major companies in one\nlocation. 盗用了 location 这个原词，但录音从未说基线组看到『同一地点的大公司』，是\n借词造出的无中生有内容。\n借用原文：others in the same sector and location\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音里 others in the same sector and location 出现过 same sector，C 项 All\ncompanies in the same sector. 直接搬来这个短语，但它说的是比较的参照系，不是基线\n组所见，听到 same sector 就选会中招。\n借用原文：compared to others in the same sector and location\nD) 张冠李戴\nVarious aspects of a company's workforce. 描述的是多样性组看到的 information\nabout the race, gender, education, and language skills of a company's workforce，录\n音明确说基线组 with no information on diversity，D 把另一组的内容安到基线组头上。\n借用原文：information about the race, gender, education, and language skills of a\ncompany's workforce", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does the passage say participants seem to be paying attention to?", "options": ["A) Job security.", "B) Potential pay raises.", "C) Minimum base salary.", "D) Information about diversity."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Information about diversity.\n【定位】Participants seemed to be paying attention to information about diversity, and using this when\ndeciding whether to click on the listing.\n参与者似乎在关注多样性方面的信息，并在决定是否点击招聘时用上了这些信息。\n【信号】that is 收束句点明关注对象。这句由 that is 引出对前文的小结，并直接用 paying\nattention to information about diversity 说出参与者关注的对象，与题干表态短语完全对\n应，是篇章的结论落点。\n【替换】paying attention to information about diversity ↔ Information about diversity.\n提取宾语成名词短语\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 项 Job security. 在录音里从未出现，整篇只谈多样性信息与薪资，没有任何关于工作稳\n定性的内容，属凭印象添加的未提及项。\nB) 过度推断\n录音多次提到 lower salaries，B 项 Potential pay raises. 借薪资话题推出『可能的加\n薪』，但录音只说参与者愿接受低薪、并未说他们关注加薪，是顺着薪资词过度推断。\n借用原文：listings with lower salaries\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 项 Minimum base salary. 借用了录音反复出现的薪资话题，但录音从未提『最低底薪』\n这一概念，更没说参与者关注它，是用薪资原词拼出的偷换项。\n借用原文：listings with lower salaries", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the passage say about the findings of a new study from Oregon State University?", "options": ["A) Positive.", "B) Original.", "C) Important.", "D) Surprising."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Important.\n【定位】The findings are important because there is a growing body of evidence that dogs can help\nchildren in many ways.\n这些发现很重要，因为越来越多的证据表明狗能在许多方面帮助孩子。\n【信号】对 findings 的直接定性。录音紧跟着研究结果给出定性 The findings are important，主\n语正是题干问的 findings，是一句直接的\"X 是怎样的\"判断句，这种直接定性句就是评价类\n细节题的答案落点。\n【替换】The findings are important ↔ Important.\n原词直接对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音第三段确实出现 positive impacts，但那是形容狗对孩子生活的影响，不是形容这项研\n究的 findings，把 Positive. 安到 findings 上属答非所问。\n借用原文：dogs can have a lot of positive impacts on a child's life\nB) 未提及\nOriginal. 表示\"首创、原创的\"，录音通篇没有对 findings 作此评价，属于无中生有。\nD) 未提及\nSurprising. 是\"出人意料的\"，录音只说 findings 重要，没有任何\"惊讶/意外\"的措辞，属无\n中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does a growing body of evidence show according to the passage?", "options": ["A) Dogs match their behavior with kids as much as with adults.", "B) Kids and adults alike find in dogs a source of attachment.", "C) Kids' attitude to dogs is influenced by adults.", "D) Dogs can help kids in many ways."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Dogs can help kids in many ways.\n【定位】there is a growing body of evidence that dogs can help children in many ways.\n越来越多的证据表明狗能在许多方面帮助孩子。\n【信号】evidence that 引出结论。录音 a growing body of evidence that dogs can help\nchildren in many ways 中的 that 直接引出证据的内容，这种\"evidence that + 句子\"结构\n里 that 后面就是答案落点。\n【替换】dogs can help children in many ways ↔ Dogs can help kids in many ways.\nchildren 与 kids 同义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nDogs match their behavior with kids as much as with adults. 借用了录音里\nmatch...behavior 的说法，但本题问的是 evidence 表明什么（帮孩子），而且原文后文恰\n恰说狗匹配孩子行为\"少于\"成人，并非 as much as，与原文相反。\n借用原文：dogs are matching children's behavior, but less frequently than what we have seen\nbetween dogs and adults\nB) 原词陷阱\nKids and adults alike find in dogs a source of attachment. 套用了录音里 source of\nattachment 一词，但原文是说狗\"作为依恋来源\"帮助孩子，并没有说\"孩子和成人都把狗当\n依恋来源\"，多加了 adults，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：serving as a source of attachment in the face of changing family structures\nC) 未提及\nKids' attitude to dogs is influenced by adults. 谈的是\"成人影响孩子对狗的态度\"，录音\n通篇没有这层意思，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What did Monique Udell say their study suggests?", "options": ["A) Dogs pay much attention to the kids they live with.", "B) Dogs view both adults and kids as social companions.", "C) Kids differ from adults in their behavior towards dogs.", "D) Kids need to be aware of the risks in playing with dogs."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Dogs pay much attention to the kids they live with.\n【定位】This suggests that while dogs may view children as social companions, there are also some\ndifferences that we need to understand better.\n这表明，尽管狗可能把孩子视为社交伙伴，但也存在一些我们需要进一步了解的差异。\n【信号】suggests that 引出研究暗示。录音中 This suggests that while dogs may view\nchildren as social companions 用 suggests that 直接对应题干的 study suggests，这正\n是 Udell 讲话里点明\"研究暗示什么\"的句子。\n【替换】dogs may view children as social companions ↔\nDogs pay much attention to the kids they live with.\n概括上位替换\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nDogs view both adults and kids as social companions. 套用了原文 social companions\n一词，但 Udell 强调的是狗把\"孩子\"当伙伴、且和成人有差异，并非\"成人和孩子都\"一视同\n仁，加了 both...and 反而违背她说的 differences，与原文相反。\n借用原文：while dogs may view children as social companions, there are also some differences\nC) 张冠李戴\nKids differ from adults in their behavior towards dogs. 借用原文 differences 一词，但\n原文说的是\"狗匹配孩子行为少于成人\"，差异在于\"狗对孩子和对成人\"的行为，而非\"孩子和\n成人对狗\"的行为，主体被调换，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：dogs are matching children's behavior, but less frequently than what we have seen\nbetween dogs and adults\nD) 原词陷阱\nKids need to be aware of the risks in playing with dogs. 套用了第三段提到的 risks，但\n本题问 Udell 说研究\"暗示\"什么，而风险是后文另一处内容，且原文未说\"孩子需提防风\n险\"，答非所问。\n借用原文：there are also risks associated with the dog-child relationship", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does research show kids are very capable of doing?", "options": ["A) Understanding dogs.", "B) Training dogs.", "C) Taking care of dogs.", "D) Associating with dogs."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Training dogs.\n【定位】But research does show that kids are very capable of training dogs.\n但研究确实表明，孩子非常擅长训练狗。\n【信号】capable of 直接点能力。录音末句 research does show that kids are very capable of\ntraining dogs 与题干几乎逐词重合，capable of 后面的 training dogs 就是答案动作，是\n直接的能力陈述句。\n【替换】kids are very capable of training dogs ↔ Training dogs.\n动作短语直接对应\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nUnderstanding dogs. 借用了原文 understand 一词，但原文说的是\"我们（研究者）还有\n很多要去了解\"，understand 的主体是研究者而非孩子，把它套到\"孩子擅长理解狗\"属张冠\n李戴。\n借用原文：there are also some differences that we need to understand better\nC) 未提及 Taking care of dogs. 的\"照料狗\"录音通篇没有提到，属无中生有。\nD) 未提及\nAssociating with dogs. 的\"与狗交往\"虽与全文话题相关，但原文明说孩子擅长的是\ntraining（训练），没有说 associating，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) approximately", "C) assume", "D) component", "E) comprehend", "F) deteriorate", "G) equivalent", "H) journey", "I) literary", "J) performed", "K) rarely", "L) sample", "M) undermined", "N) unique", "O) unit"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) unique\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词 was 后作表语\n【依据】下一句正解释「并不罕见」\nNumerous writers produce masterpieces well into their 70s and beyond. 紧跟其后说\n明「许多作家年逾古稀仍出杰作」，正是在否定 Morrison 在大器晚成上的「独一无二\n性」，因此 And Morrison was not __(26)__ in this regard. 应为「并非独一无二的」。\n【竞争词】G) equivalent 缺少对比对象\nequivalent 表「与...等同」，须有比较对象（equivalent to sb.）；本句只给出 in this regard\n这一抽象限定，没有具体参照物，且 was not equivalent 与下一句「许多人也如此」逻辑反\n向，行不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) approximately", "C) assume", "D) component", "E) comprehend", "F) deteriorate", "G) equivalent", "H) journey", "I) literary", "J) performed", "K) rarely", "L) sample", "M) undermined", "N) unique", "O) unit"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) literary\n形容词\n【词性槽】形容词修饰名词 accomplishments\n【依据】上文全在谈作家与小说\n前文反复提到 When Toni Morrison died in 2019, the world lost one of its most\ninfluential authors. 与 Her first novel was not published until she was 39，\naccomplishments 指的就是「文学成就」，所以 literary 与上下文最贴合。\n【竞争词】G) equivalent 语义不通\nequivalent 意为「等同的」，需要与另一参照对象比较，这里并未出现可比物，「等同的成就」\n放进 Such __(27)__ accomplishments highlight an important point. 不知与什么等同，逻辑\n断裂。\nN) unique 已被 26 题选用\nunique 在解 26 题时已被划走（见 26 题），整套词库每词只能用一次，本空不再考虑。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) approximately", "C) assume", "D) component", "E) comprehend", "F) deteriorate", "G) equivalent", "H) journey", "I) literary", "J) performed", "K) rarely", "L) sample", "M) undermined", "N) unique", "O) unit"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) deteriorate\n动词\n【词性槽】to 后接动词原形\n【依据】deteriorate with age 固定搭配\ndeteriorate with age「随年龄退化」是英语中描述衰老的标准表达；Our capacity to\nspeak, write and learn new vocabulary does not seem to __(28)__ with age.「我们的\n语言能力似乎并不随年龄退化」精准契合全文主旨。\n【竞争词】E) comprehend 语义与 with age 不搭\ncomprehend 表「理解」，「不随年龄理解」语义不通；且 comprehend 是及物动词，后面缺\n少宾语；同时 29 题正需要 comprehend 与 produce 并列。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) approximately", "C) assume", "D) component", "E) comprehend", "F) deteriorate", "G) equivalent", "H) journey", "I) literary", "J) performed", "K) rarely", "L) sample", "M) undermined", "N) unique", "O) unit"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) comprehend\n动词\n【词性槽】to produce and to ___ 并列结构\n【依据】produce 与 comprehend 对仗\nproduce language（产出语言）与 comprehend language（理解语言）构成语言能力的\n「输出—输入」标准对子，是语言学领域常用搭配；our ability to produce and to\n__(29)__ language is well preserved into older adulthood. 顺势承接主旨「老年仍保留\n语言能力」。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) approximately", "C) assume", "D) component", "E) comprehend", "F) deteriorate", "G) equivalent", "H) journey", "I) literary", "J) performed", "K) rarely", "L) sample", "M) undermined", "N) unique", "O) unit"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) journey\n名词\n【词性槽】is a ___ that 主表结构\n【依据】把语言习得比作终身旅程\nlanguage mastery is a __(30)__ that we begin as infants and continue on for the rest\nof our lives.，begin... continue... for the rest of our lives 正是 journey 的典型语境（人\n生/学习的「旅程」），与「终身学习」的喻体完全匹配。\n【竞争词】D) component 组成部分 ≠ 终身过程\ncomponent 表「组成部分」，与「从婴儿开始持续一生」的连续性语义不搭——人不会「开始\n并持续一生地做一个 component」。\nO) unit 单位/单元静态\nunit 指静态的「单位/单元」，无法承载「begin and continue for life」这一动态过程，搭配\n不当。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) approximately", "C) assume", "D) component", "E) comprehend", "F) deteriorate", "G) equivalent", "H) journey", "I) literary", "J) performed", "K) rarely", "L) sample", "M) undermined", "N) unique", "O) unit"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) actually\n副词\n【词性槽】情态结构里修饰动词\n【依据】强调「与预期相反」\n全文核心论点是「语言能力不随年龄衰退」，本句进一步给出强例证：词义知识在中老年还\n会提高，与「人老智衰」的常识相反，正好用 actually「实际上」来强调反预期；与下一句\nOne study, for example, found that adults over sixty had an average vocabulary size\nof over 21,000 words. 的举例链衔接通顺。\n【竞争词】K) rarely 与论点反向\nrarely improve「很少提升」会让本句意思翻转为「中老年词汇几乎不提升」，与下一句「60 岁\n以上成人词汇量超 21,000 词」的研究例证、以及全文「语言能力不衰退」主旨直接矛盾。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 K) —— actually vs rarely 反向陷阱。knowledge of word\nmeanings, __(31)__ improve，rarely 词性与 actually 完全一致，光看语法位置无法区分；\n不少考生若没读到下一句的研究数据，会想当然填 rarely「很少提升」与「人老智衰」的常\n识匹配，正中陷阱。防坑：本题副词类二选一时先回全文找主旨，再决定方向。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) approximately", "C) assume", "D) component", "E) comprehend", "F) deteriorate", "G) equivalent", "H) journey", "I) literary", "J) performed", "K) rarely", "L) sample", "M) undermined", "N) unique", "O) unit"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) sample\n名词\n【词性槽】studied 的宾语 a ___ of...\n【依据】研究语境的「样本」\n上下文是 The researchers also studied a __(32)__ of college students，researcher +\nstudied 已经标明这是科研场景，a sample of college students「一份大学生样本」是研\n究文献的固定表达。\n【竞争词】D) component 搭配不成立\ncomponent 指「组成部分」，常说 a component of a system，与 college students 搭配生\n硬；研究语境里也极少说「一组学生组件」。\nH) journey 需留给 30 题的「过程」\njourney 在此句搭配 a journey of college students 不通；同时本词在 30 题（语言学习是一段\n历程）有更贴切的归宿，先做的 32 题不应抢用它。\nO) unit 搭配语义不准\na unit of college students 听起来像「一队学生」，多用于军事/组织编制，而非研究取样语\n境；研究里通常说 sample。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) approximately", "C) assume", "D) component", "E) comprehend", "F) deteriorate", "G) equivalent", "H) journey", "I) literary", "J) performed", "K) rarely", "L) sample", "M) undermined", "N) unique", "O) unit"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) approximately\n副词\n【词性槽】数字 16,000 前需副词修饰\n【依据】approximately + 数字是常用搭配\ncontained __(33)__ 16,000 words，「大约 16,000 个单词」对应研究里给出的平均值估\n算，搭配自然、语义合理。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) approximately", "C) assume", "D) component", "E) comprehend", "F) deteriorate", "G) equivalent", "H) journey", "I) literary", "J) performed", "K) rarely", "L) sample", "M) undermined", "N) unique", "O) unit"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) performed\n动词\n【词性槽】主语后缺谓语动词\n【依据】performed better 是典型搭配\nperform 在科研语境中常表「在测试/任务中的表现」，performed better than\nparticipants in their youth or middle years on tasks 是英语固定搭配 perform better\non tasks「在任务上表现更好」，语义、时态均贴合。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) actually", "B) approximately", "C) assume", "D) component", "E) comprehend", "F) deteriorate", "G) equivalent", "H) journey", "I) literary", "J) performed", "K) rarely", "L) sample", "M) undermined", "N) unique", "O) unit"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) assume\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 might 后接动词原形\n【依据】contrary to what people assume 固定逻辑\ncontrary to what many might __(35)__ 是英语经典「与人们想当然的看法相反」句式，\nassume 表「想当然地以为」最贴合；与后面 does not weaken with age 形成「常识 vs\n事实」对照，呼应 overview 反预期立意。\n【竞争词】E) comprehend 要留给 29 题\ncomprehend 在 29 题与 produce 并列搭配 produce and to ___ language，是该空唯一通顺\n选择；本空若先抢走，会让 29 题无解。同时本句语义重点是「设想/猜想」，不是「理解」。\nF) deteriorate 要留给 28 题\ndeteriorate 是 28 题「does not seem to ___ with age」的固定答案；放到本空 what many\nmight deteriorate 语法不通（deteriorate 通常作不及物动词），既错位又抢走了 28 题的唯一\n选择。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 E) —— comprehend 错位抢词。contrary to what many might\n__(35)__, does not weaken with age，comprehend 与 might 语法上完全搭得上，「与\n许多人可能理解的相反」乍看也通，但它会抢走 29 题唯一的答案；并且本句核心是「想当\n然」而非「理解」。防坑：定动词时不仅看本空通不通，也要扫一眼别的空有没有更需要这\n个词的位置。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "According to a management expert, work-life balance is not as simple as giving equal amounts of time to work and personal life.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】管理学专家观点+工作生活时间不能简单等分\n【定位】But the problem is that we often tell ourselves: \"I'm going to put in eight hours' worth of\nwork, and then I'm going to put in eight hours' worth of 'me time', which will include my\nfamily, my hobbies, my workout, my everything,\" says Anat Lechner, clinical associate\nprofessor of management at New York University. \"I don't think it's such a simple formula.\"\n但问题在于，我们常常对自己说：\"我要投入八小时来工作，然后再投入八小时给\"自己的时\n间\"，包括家人、爱好、锻炼、一切。\"纽约大学管理学临床副教授 Anat Lechner 说。\"我不\n认为它是一个这么简单的公式。\"\n【改写】According to a management expert ↔\nAnat Lechner, clinical associate professor of management at New York University\n身份概括上位\nnot as simple as giving equal amounts of time to work and personal life ↔\neight hours' worth of work, and then I'm going to put in eight hours' worth of 'me time'\n具体公式被概括为等量时间分配\nnot as simple as ↔ I don't think it's such a simple formula\n否定结论同义改写", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Research found that those who are given greater flexibility at work are less stressed and more likely to stay in their jobs.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】更大灵活性+压力更小+更愿留任\n【定位】Unsurprisingly, Kelly and Moen found that employees in the redesign group reported less\nstress and less burnout. They were less likely to quit their jobs; indeed, over the next four\nyears, they were 40% less likely to quit than those who kept working under the old policies.\n不出意外，Kelly 和 Moen 发现，重新设计组的员工报告称压力更小、倦怠更少。他们更不\n容易离职；事实上，在接下来的四年里，他们离职的可能性比那些仍在旧政策下工作的员工\n低 40%。\n【改写】given greater flexibility at work ↔ giving workers greater flexibility\n几乎逐字同义\nare less stressed ↔ less stress and less burnout\n形容词与名词同根改写\nmore likely to stay in their jobs ↔\n40% less likely to quit than those who kept working under the old policies\n正话反说\n【辨邻】 I I 段只说 split more than 1,000 employees at a Fortune 500 company into two\ngroups 这种实验设置，未提到\"压力更小\"\"更愿留任\"，因此尽管同样涉及灵活性话\n题，也只能算邻段，不是源头段。\nshe and co-author Phyllis Moen split more than 1,000 employees at a Fortune 500 company into\ntwo groups, one that worked under a management redesign and one that continued working within\nthe existing management structure", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Workers who rejected working long hours tended to make regular adjustments in order to achieve work-life balance.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】拒绝加班者+常态化调整\n【定位】Lupu and Ruiz-Castro looked at those who rejected the long hours and they found that those\nworkers actually had strikingly similar strategies for maintaining their work-life balance.\nThey had a tendency to reflect and question assumptions in the name of self-awareness and\nregularly took steps to adjust the things standing in their way to work-life balance.\nLupu 和 Ruiz-Castro 研究了那些拒绝长时间工作的人，发现这些人维持工作-生活平衡的策\n略惊人地相似。他们倾向于在自我觉察的名义下反思并质疑既有假设，并经常采取行动去调\n整阻碍他们实现工作-生活平衡的因素。\n【改写】Workers who rejected working long hours ↔ those who rejected the long hours\n近乎逐字同义\nmake regular adjustments in order to achieve work-life balance ↔\nregularly took steps to adjust the things standing in their way to work-life balance\n副词转形容词的等值改写\n【辨邻】 F F 段 five steps 与逐条罗列 paused and reconsidered beliefs 是\"方法的具体细化\"；\n陈述句没有提到\"五步\"也没有提到具体步骤，只说\"定期调整\"，因此匹配的是 E 段的概\n括，不是 F 段的清单。\nLupu and Ruiz-Castro identified five steps that the respondents in the study who had better work-\nlife balance used in their jobs\nD D 段 about 30% of the men and 50% of the women reported resisting working\nlong hours 只在分类陈述群体，未提及他们\"定期调整\"等策略，不能承载陈述句的核\n心信息。\nabout 30% of the men and 50% of the women reported resisting working long hours", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Talking about work-life balance is said to be a privilege reserved for the better-off, not for those who barely make a living.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】特权对话+富裕者vs糊口者\n【定位】For millions of people, work is about being able to put food on the table. Talking about work-\nlife balance \"is a very privileged conversation\", says Lechner. \"If we're reflecting, maybe we\nshould also reflect on that.\"\n对于数百万人来说，工作就是为了能把饭端上桌。谈论工作-生活平衡，\"是一场非常特权化\n的对话\"，Lechner 说。\"如果我们在反思，或许我们也该反思一下这一点。\"\n【改写】a privilege reserved for the better-off ↔\nTalking about work-life balance \"is a very privileged conversation\"\n同根词的名词与形容词改写\nnot for those who barely make a living ↔\nFor millions of people, work is about being able to put food on the table\n习语意义改写", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Knowing one's emotional state is of utmost importance in deciding what changes to make for a better work-life balance.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】了解情绪状态+最关键+决定改变\n【定位】\"Awareness of your emotional state is essential in order to determine the changes you want to\nmake in your work and in your life,\" says Lupu.\n\"对自身情绪状态的觉察，对于决定你想在工作与生活中做出什么改变是至关重要\n的，\"Lupu 说。\n【改写】Knowing one's emotional state ↔ Awareness of your emotional state\n动名词与名词同义改写\nis of utmost importance ↔ is essential\n近义形容词与名词短语等值\nin deciding what changes to make for a better work-life balance ↔\nin order to determine the changes you want to make in your work and in your life\n目的状语等值改写\n【辨邻】 F F 段 they zeroed in on their resultant emotions 只是五步中的第二步，没有说情绪\n觉察\"至关重要\"，没有承载\"of utmost importance\"这层定性。\nSecond, after identifying the cause, they zeroed in on their resultant emotions\nH H 段 underlying emotional sources of stress are still there 描述情绪未处理的后\n果，没有提到\"了解情绪状态对决定改变最重要\"这一定性论断。\nIf the underlying emotional sources of stress are still there, then the time you actually spend at\nhome may not be enjoyable", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "More female professionals reported being reluctant to work overtime than their male counterparts.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】女性比男性更多人不愿加班\n【定位】Although it sounds like the respondents had a lot in common, here's what separated them:\nabout 30% of the men and 50% of the women reported resisting working long hours.\n尽管受访者听起来有很多共同点，但区别他们的关键是：大约 30% 的男性和 50% 的女性表\n示自己抗拒长时间工作。\n【改写】More female professionals reported being reluctant to work overtime than their male counterparts\n↔ about 30% of the men and 50% of the women reported resisting working long hours\n数字大小关系转比较级\nbeing reluctant to work overtime ↔ resisting working long hours\n近义词同义替换\n【辨邻】 E E 段 those who rejected the long hours 只是接着 D 段往下讲他们的策略，但没有\n出现 30% 或 50% 这种数字，也没有出现男女对比的字眼，不能承载本题男女比例对\n比的信息。\nLupu and Ruiz-Castro looked at those who rejected the long hours and they found that those\nworkers actually had strikingly similar strategies", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Without organisational support, any personal effort to maintain work-life balance will be unsuccessful.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】缺乏组织支持+个人努力徒劳\n【定位】If your workplace isn't an environment where work-life balance is possible in the first place,\nany effort you attempt to make toward it on a personal level will be in vain.\n如果你的工作场所本身就不是一个能让工作-生活平衡得以实现的环境，那么你在个人层面\n上做出的任何努力都将徒劳无功。\n【改写】Without organisational support ↔\nIf your workplace isn't an environment where work-life balance is possible in the first place\nif not 等价 without\nany personal effort to maintain work-life balance will be unsuccessful ↔\nany effort you attempt to make toward it on a personal level will be in vain\nin vain 与 unsuccessful 同义\n【辨邻】 I I 段 companies and managers can play a key role in creating a better\nenvironment for workers 只说组织可以起作用，没有给出\"如果组织不支持，个人努\n力将徒劳\"的反向结论。\ncompanies and managers can play a key role in creating a better environment for workers\nJ J 段 employees in the redesign group reported less stress and less burnout 是数\n据结论，没有出现\"any effort…will be in vain\"这种条件式徒劳判断。\nUnsurprisingly, Kelly and Moen found that employees in the redesign group reported less stress and\nless burnout", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "The question of how to achieve work-life balance has long been the main subject of discussion among workers.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】长期讨论+主要话题\n【定位】Few topics have been so endlessly analysed as work-life balance. The quest to attain\nthis mysterious state has dominated discussion around careers for years—especially for\nworking parents.\n很少有话题像工作-生活平衡这样被无休止地反复分析。多年来，追求这种神秘状态的努力\n一直主导着围绕职业的讨论——尤其是在职父母中间。\n【改写】The question of how to achieve work-life balance ↔ The quest to attain this mysterious state\n同义短语替换\nhas long been the main subject of discussion among workers ↔\nhas dominated discussion around careers for years\n动词与名词短语的等值改写\n【辨邻】 C C 段 Some researchers are now encouraging us to stop thinking about work-\nlife balance as an achievement 是新研究角度，没有出现\"长期/主要话题\"这种总起\n判断。\nSome researchers are now encouraging us to stop thinking about work-life balance as an\nachievement that you either hit or don't", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "You may not actually experience emotional wellbeing at home if you remain occupied with what happened at work.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】在家但心还在工作+无法享受\n【定位】If the underlying emotional sources of stress are still there, then the time you actually spend\nat home may not be enjoyable. \"We come home and even though physically we are there,\nmentally we still may be processing things that happened at work. We're not present,\"\nshe says.\n如果潜在的情绪压力源仍然存在，那么你真正待在家里的时间也可能并不愉快。\"我们回到\n家，即便身体在那里，心里可能仍然在处理工作发生的事情。我们其实并不在场，\"她说。\n【改写】may not actually experience emotional wellbeing at home ↔\nthe time you actually spend at home may not be enjoyable\n近义概念替换\nif you remain occupied with what happened at work ↔\nmentally we still may be processing things that happened at work\n比喻句的直陈改写\n【辨邻】 B B 段 I'm going to put in eight hours' worth of work, and then I'm going to put\nin eight hours' worth of 'me time' 只是讨论时间公式，没有出现\"身体在心理不\n在\"这种意象。\nI'm going to put in eight hours' worth of work, and then I'm going to put in eight hours' worth of 'me\ntime', which will include my family, my hobbies, my workout, my everything", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Some researchers suggest that work-life balance is not a goal to achieve, but a process for life to be adjusted promptly.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】不是要达成的目标+是要随时调整的过程\n【定位】Some researchers are now encouraging us to stop thinking about work-life balance as an\nachievement that you either hit or don't. Instead, they suggest it may be more of a lifelong\nprocess—a continuous, never-ending exercise that requires self-awareness and timely\nadjustments. Researchers Ioana Lupu and Mayra Ruiz-Castro argue that work-life balance is\n\"a cycle, not an achievement\".\n一些研究者现在鼓励我们不再把工作-生活平衡视为一种要么达成、要么没达成的成就。相\n反，他们认为它更可能是一个终身的过程——一个需要自我觉察与适时调整、持续不断的实\n践。研究者 Ioana Lupu 和 Mayra Ruiz-Castro 主张：工作-生活平衡\"是一个循环，不是一\n种成就\"。\n【改写】not a goal to achieve ↔ not an achievement\n近义词替换\nbut a process for life to be adjusted promptly ↔\na lifelong process—a continuous, never-ending exercise that requires self-awareness and timely\nadjustments\n近义词替换+定语改写\n【辨邻】 B B 段 I don't think it's such a simple formula 只是否定简单公式，没有正面提出\"它\n是一种循环或过程\"这种重新定义。\nI don't think it's such a simple formula\nH H 段 What we call \"work-life balance\" is actually just a substitute to having a\nsense of fulfillment and contentment 是\"替代品\"视角，没有出现\"过程或循环\"这种\n重新定义。\nWhat we call \"work-life balance\" is actually just a substitute to having a sense of fulfillment\nand contentment", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the author want to emphasise in the first paragraph?", "options": ["A) The year 2020 was a big turning point in human history.", "B) Economic growth benefits people little in the long run.", "C) It is unwise to weaken the human connection to nature.", "D) It is harmful to mankind to use human-made materials."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题）\n【判型】What does the author want to emphasise in the first paragraph? 问的是首段作者想强\n调什么，是段落主旨而非局部细节。\n【拆句】What does the author want to emphasise in the first paragraph? 是 What 引导的特殊\n疑问句，want to emphasise 是一个简单的动词短语，无从句无否定，结构清爽，无陷阱。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nThe year 2020 was a big turning point in human history. 把段落里一个支撑论据\n（2020 年的 tipping point）当作主旨，且\"in human history\"是过度推断——原文只\n说人造物质量超过生命这一比较点。\nB) 偷换概念\nEconomic growth benefits people little in the long run. 把原文\"对经济或许有利\"扭\n曲成\"经济增长对人没好处\"，攻击点从\"与自然的连接\"偷换到了\"经济增长本身\"，并把短\n期权衡偷换为 in the long run。\nC) ✓ 正确\nThe weakening of the human connection to nature might be good for economic growth but is\nbad for people.\n↔ It is unwise to weaken the human connection to nature.\nContinuing to grow concrete forests rather than real ones is shortsighted. ↔\nIt is unwise to weaken the human connection to nature.\n预判要的是\"削弱与自然的连接是不明智的\"，It is unwise to weaken the human\nconnection to nature. 正好是这一判断的同义表达，且覆盖整段而不偏向某个论据。\nD) 过度推断\nIt is harmful to mankind to use human-made materials. 把\"人造物总质量超过生\n命\"这一比较事实，推断成\"使用人造物本身就有害\"，原文从未否定使用人造物，只是反\n对以此取代真实自然。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What did researchers find about popular culture?", "options": ["A) It is increasingly detached from the natural world.", "B) It is filled with all kinds of video game characters.", "C) It is especially appealing to the taste of teenagers.", "D) It is still impacted by the hit songs of the 1950s."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What did researchers find about popular culture? 直接问研究者发现了什么客观事实，\n属于细节题。\n【拆句】What did researchers find about popular culture? 是 What 引导的特殊疑问句，主谓\nresearchers find，about popular culture 限定 find 的对象，结构平铺，无陷阱。\n【定位】For every three nature-related words in hit songs of the 1950s, researchers found, there was\nonly slightly more than one 50 years later.\n对于 1950 年代流行歌曲里每三个与自然相关的词，研究人员发现，50 年后只剩下略多于\n一个。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nFor every three nature-related words in hit songs of the 1950s, researchers found, there was only\nslightly more than one 50 years later.\n↔ It is increasingly detached from the natural world.\nYet slipping from popular culture is the wonder and beauty of the natural world. ↔\nIt is increasingly detached from the natural world.\n预判要的是\"自然内容大量减少\"，A 项用 increasingly detached 这一抽象升级精确对\n应该数据，所以锁定 A。\nB) 偷换概念\nIt is filled with all kinds of video game characters. 把原文\"孩子认识的游戏角色比野\n生动物多\"偷换成\"流行文化里充斥着游戏角色\"——研究比较的是孩子的认知，不是流行\n文化的内容构成。\nC) 无中生有\nIt is especially appealing to the taste of teenagers. 全句\"对青少年口味格外有吸引\n力\"在 P2 中没有任何对应；原文只是提到青少年将能参加自然史考试，并未谈流行文化\n对青少年的吸引力。\nD) 与原文相反\nIt is still impacted by the hit songs of the 1950s. 说流行文化仍受 50 年代流行歌曲\n影响，但原文用 50 年代和 50 年后的数据对比，恰恰说明这种影响在减弱、自然主题在\n流失，方向相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does the author propose people do?", "options": ["A) Take measures to ensure sustainable food development.", "B) Reconnect with nature through the right to grow.", "C) Stand by the parliament in fighting social diseases.", "D) Cover public spaces with fruit trees and vegetable plants."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author propose people do? 问作者具体提议人们做什么，定位到对应主\n张句即可作答，属于细节题。\n【拆句】What does the author propose people do? 是 What 引导的特殊疑问句，propose 后\n接\"people do\"是\"propose sb do sth\"句型省略了 that 与情态的简略问法，相当于问\"作者\n建议人们做什么\"，无陷阱。\n【定位】Part of remedying this social disease would be for parliament to pass a \"right to grow\" law,\nallowing anyone to turn underused public spaces into vegetable and fruit gardens. The idea is\nfor people to get back in touch with the soil—while producing food sustainably.\n矫正这一社会病的一部分举措，是让议会通过一项\"种植权\"法案，允许任何人把利用不足的\n公共空间改造成菜园和果园。其理念在于让人们重新接触土壤——同时可持续地生产粮食。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nTake measures to ensure sustainable food development. 把原文末尾\"while\nproducing food sustainably\"这一附带的从属信息抬升成了核心主张，掩盖了\"重新接\n触土壤、与自然重连\"这一真正的主诉。\nB) ✓ 正确\nPart of remedying this social disease would be for parliament to pass a \"right to grow\" law ↔\nReconnect with nature through the right to grow.\nThe idea is for people to get back in touch with the soil ↔\nReconnect with nature through the right to grow.\n预判要的两层信息——\"通过种植权\"和\"重新接触自然\"——B 项用 through 和\nreconnect with nature 一并覆盖，所以是答案。\nC) 偷换概念\nStand by the parliament in fighting social diseases. 把\"议会通过法案\"偷换成\"人\n们站在议会一边\"，方向完全反过来——原文是作者建议议会行动，不是建议民众站队\n议会。\nD) 过度推断\nCover public spaces with fruit trees and vegetable plants. 把原文\"allowing\nanyone to turn underused public spaces\"的\"利用不足的公共空间\"扩展到\"所有公共\n空间\"，并把\"允许\"扭曲为\"全面覆盖\"，方式与范围都被放大。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about vegetable planting?", "options": ["A) It all started in 1649 in Southern England.", "B) It is protected by US government regulations.", "C) It has long been used to increase food supply and improve urban landscape.", "D) It has been popularised worldwide with the increasing influence of social media."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the passage about vegetable planting? 问从文章中获取的事实\n信息，属于细节题。\n【拆句】What do we learn from the passage about vegetable planting? 主干 What do we\nlearn，from the passage 与 about vegetable planting 都是介词短语作状语，前者点出\n来源、后者点出话题对象，结构清晰。\n【定位】In April 1649, locals responded to high prices and food shortages by cultivating vegetables on\ncommon land in Southern England. The practice of throwing seed bombs to turn vacant plots\nof land green took off in 1970s New York, and has been revived by green-thumbed social\nmedia influencers who defy local US regulations in a war on ugly spots in cities.\n1649 年 4 月，英国南部居民为应对高物价与粮食短缺，在公共土地上种植蔬菜。把种子炸\n弹扔到空地以使其变绿的做法在 1970 年代的纽约兴起，并被有园艺天分的社交媒体网红重\n新带火，他们无视当地美国法规，向城市里的丑陋角落\"开战\"。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nIt all started in 1649 in Southern England. 把段内一个时间点放大为\"全部起源\"，原\n文只说 1649 年发生过一次，并未说蔬菜种植起源于此。\nB) 与原文相反\nIt is protected by US government regulations. 与原文 defy local US regulations 直\n接相反：原文说网红\"无视\"美国当地法规，选项说\"受其保护\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nlocals responded to high prices and food shortages by cultivating vegetables on common land in\nSouthern England\n↔ It has long been used to increase food supply and improve urban landscape.\nthrowing seed bombs to turn vacant plots of land green ↔\nIt has long been used to increase food supply and improve urban landscape.\nVegetable planting has a respectable tradition. ↔\nIt has long been used to increase food supply and improve urban landscape.\n预判要的\"补食物 + 美化城市\"两条线，C 项用 food supply 与 urban landscape 并\n列，正好两条都覆盖，所以是答案。\nD) 过度推断\nIt has been popularised worldwide with the increasing influence of social media.\n把原文里\"1970 年代纽约 + 美国网红重新带火\"扩张为\"全球普及\"，并把\"重振\"延伸\n为\"日益增长的影响力\"，超出原文范围。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What can publicly accessible fruit and vegetable gardens do apart from their practical functions?", "options": ["A) Raise people's environmental awareness.", "B) Add to the great variety of plant species.", "C) Act as the first defence against natural disasters.", "D) Enhance people's community spirit."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What can publicly accessible fruit and vegetable gardens do apart from their\npractical functions? 问的是除实用功能之外这些菜园还能做什么，是对应原文并列项的细\n节题。\n【拆句】What can publicly accessible fruit and vegetable gardens do apart from their\npractical functions? 主干 What can ... gardens do，apart from their practical\nfunctions 作状语限定范围，相当于把\"实用功能\"这一已知项排除出去；其中 publicly\naccessible 是副词修饰形容词，整体修饰 gardens。\n【定位】They can make unlovely spaces lovely, and marry use and beauty as well as help promote a\nsense of community.\n它们能化丑为美，把实用与美感融合在一起，并有助于增强一种社区感。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nRaise people's environmental awareness. 把\"连接食物与产地、对抗物种丧失\"偷换\n成\"提升环保意识\"，前者是行为层面的连接和效用，后者是心理认知层面的意识。\nB) 无中生有\nAdd to the great variety of plant species. 说菜园会增加植物物种多样性，原文从未\n谈\"增加植物物种多样性\"这一功能。\nC) 偷换概念\nAct as the first defence against natural disasters. 把 first defence against species\nloss and climate change 偷换成\"对抗自然灾害\"——物种丧失和气候变化不等于\"自然\n灾害\"，并且选项主语 act as 暗示把整个菜园当成防线，与原文 Plants are 也对不上。\nD) ✓ 正确\nhelp promote a sense of community ↔ Enhance people's community spirit.\n预判要的\"增强社区感\"在原文里有直接对应的 \"help promote a sense of\ncommunity\"，D 项把 promote 替换为 enhance、sense of community 替换为\ncommunity spirit，是高保真同义替换，可锁定。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the passage say about the engineering profession in the United States?", "options": ["A) It has seen a change in attitude towards women engineers since 1979.", "B) It witnessed a significant increase in women engineers in the 1970s.", "C) It has experienced the gradual weakening of male dominance.", "D) It boasted the largest number of engineering majors in 2000."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage say about the engineering profession in the United States 没\n有 infer/imply/mainly about 等推断或主旨标志，问 \"the passage say\"，是对原文已陈述\n事实的核对，属四级最高频的细节题。\n【拆句】题干主干是 What does the passage say about ...，是 say about 后接宾语的特殊疑问\n句，结构平铺直叙、无陷阱，只要知道 say about=\"就……说了什么\"即可。\n【定位】In 1970, the percentage of women majoring in engineering was less than 1%. In 1979, that\nnumber was 9%. Many hoped women would continue to enter the field at the same rate. But\nthat's not what happened.\n1970 年，主修工程的女性占比不到 1%；1979 年这一数字升至 9%。许多人本希望女性能\n以同样速度持续进入这一行业，但事实并非如此。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文只给比例数据，没有提任何\"对待女工程师态度的变化\"，a change in attitude\ntowards women engineers since 1979 属于凭空添加新概念。\nB) ✓ 正确\nIn 1970, the percentage of women majoring in engineering was less than 1%. In 1979, that\nnumber was 9%\n↔ It witnessed a significant increase in women engineers in the 1970s\nIn 1970 和 In 1979 ↔ in the 1970s\n预判要的是\"70 年代女工程师比例大幅上升\"，B 项 significant increase in women\nengineers in the 1970s 几乎逐词对应，无需犹豫即可锁定。\nC) 与原文相反\nToday, only 21% of engineering majors are women, a number largely unchanged\nsince 2000 明确说自 2000 年起男性主导未弱化，与 gradual weakening of male\ndominance 直接相反。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 2000 年只是\"21% 未变\"的起算点，并未说 2000 年工程专业人数达到最大，\nboasted the largest number of engineering majors in 2000 属于把时间词原样搬，\n结论却换了内容。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does one survey taker say was her greatest challenge?", "options": ["A) Not to feel superior to less qualified male engineers.", "B) Not to take seriously all the messages she was getting.", "C) Not to think highly of her qualifications when promoted or given a pay raise.", "D) Not to lose self-confidence though constantly discouraged or unfairly treated."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does one survey taker say was her greatest challenge 是对原文一句引语的客观\n还原，属细节题。\n【拆句】What does one survey taker say was her greatest challenge 是个嵌套问句，say 后面\n接的是 \"(that) her greatest challenge was ____\"，疑问词 What 提到了句首，回原文找\n\"挑战是 ____\" 这层语义即可。\n【定位】The greatest challenge for me was continuing to believe in myself, when all the messages I\nwas getting were that I would never be taken seriously or promoted or given raises at the\nsame rate as men, who were clearly less qualified and not as smart as I was.\n我最大的挑战是在收到\"我永远不会像男性那样被认真对待、被晋升或被加薪——而那些男\n性明显不如我合格、不如我聪明\"这类信息时，仍然继续相信自己。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文受访者只是说\"那些男性明显不如我合格\"，是事实陈述；Not to feel superior to\nless qualified male engineers 把它扭曲成\"不要觉得自己高人一等\"，方向截然不同。\nB) 答非所问\n原文 all the messages I was getting 是\"挑战的背景\"，不是\"挑战本身\"；B 项却把\"不\n要把这些信息当真\"说成挑战，错把背景当主体。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文说\"她被晋升/加薪的待遇不如男性\"，C 项却说\"在被晋升/加薪时不要看高自己的资\n历\"，既颠倒了情境（她没有被同等晋升），也错读了态度。\nD) ✓ 正确\ncontinuing to believe in myself ↔ Not to lose self-confidence\nall the messages I was getting were that I would never be taken seriously or promoted or given\nraises at the same rate as men\n↔ though constantly discouraged or unfairly treated\n预判要\"在打压中坚持自信\"，D 项 Not to lose self-confidence though constantly\ndiscouraged or unfairly treated 两个要素全有，是定位句的精准同义替换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "How do women engineers frequently feel according to the two civil engineers?", "options": ["A) Disqualified on many levels.", "B) Excluded from the group.", "C) Overworked by their organizations.", "D) Looked down upon by male colleagues."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】How do women engineers frequently feel according to the two civil engineers 问的\n是\"她们感觉如何\"，依据 according to 直接锁定原文两位 civil engineer 的话，是细节归\n纳题。\n【拆句】How do women engineers frequently feel according to the two civil engineers 主干\n是 How do women engineers feel，frequently 是状语，according to ... 是介词短语作\n状语，限定信息来源，没有从句也无否定，是直白的特殊疑问句。\n【定位】A civil engineer said, \"We are 'women engineers.' People don't refer to a man as a 'man\nengineer'—he's an engineer. We are constantly reminded that we don't truly belong.\" Another\ncivil engineer stated, \"On many levels, you're never quite one of the groups.\"\n一位土木工程师说：\"我们被叫作'女工程师'。人们不会把男人叫'男工程师'——他就是工程\n师。我们时刻被提醒：我们并不真正属于这里。\"另一位土木工程师说：\"在很多层面上，你\n从来不算这个圈子的一员。\"\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文 On many levels, you're never quite one of the groups 中的 on many levels 修\n饰\"在多个层面上\"不属于群体，A 项 Disqualified on many levels 把它说成\"在多个层\n面上不合格\"，是把\"不属于群体\"换成\"不合格\"。\nB) ✓ 正确\nWe are constantly reminded that we don't truly belong ↔ Excluded from the group\nyou're never quite one of the groups ↔ Excluded from the group\n预判要\"不属于/被排除的情绪\"，B 项 Excluded from the group 直接对应两位受访者的\n核心情感，是最稳的同义替换。\nC) 张冠李戴\nP6 全段谈的是\"不属于群体\"的归属感问题，根本未涉及工作量；Overworked by their\norganizations 是从 work twice as hard 借来的概念，硬塞进了 53 题。\nD) 过度推断\n原文两位土木工程师讲的是\"被整个圈子提醒不属于\"，并未具体说\"是男同事看低自己\"；\nLooked down upon by male colleagues 把抽象的群体压缩成\"男同事\"这一具体施\n动者。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What probably makes young women engineers more accepted nowadays?", "options": ["A) Their success in gaining an equal footing.", "B) Their ability to balance work and family.", "C) The change in their responsibilities.", "D) The increase in their number."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What probably makes young women engineers more accepted nowadays 看似含\nprobably 像推断，实质问\"原文里给出的原因是什么\"，原文已经直说理由，仍是细节题。\n【拆句】What probably makes young women engineers more accepted nowadays 是 What\nmakes sb. + 形容词 的使役结构，What 作主语，makes 是谓语，young women\nengineers 是宾语，more accepted nowadays 是宾语补足语，意为\"是什么让年轻女工程\n师如今更被接受\"。\n【定位】Young women engineers are more accepted mostly because there are just more of them.\n年轻女工程师之所以如今更被接受，主要是因为她们的人数变多了。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\n原文 Young women engineers are on an equal footing until they have children 只\n说\"育前与男性平起平坐\"，是过去就存在的状态，不是\"如今更被接受\"的新原因；A 项\nTheir success in gaining an equal footing 把它包装成新成就，归错原因。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文 then they struggle to balance work and family 明明说\"在生育后她们挣扎于平\n衡工作与家庭\"，B 项却说\"她们有能力平衡工作与家庭\"，方向完全相反。\nC) 无中生有\n原文 compete with men who don't have the same household responsibilities 提\n到\"家庭责任不对等\"，但从未说女工程师的责任发生了变化；C 项 The change in their\nresponsibilities 是凭空增添一个变化。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthere are just more of them ↔ The increase in their number\n预判要\"人数变多\"，D 项 The increase in their number 是这层意思的最贴近名词化表\n达，无可争议。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What can we conclude about many female engineers from the statement of a mechanical engineer?", "options": ["A) They take great pride in their chosen career.", "B) They have reaped the benefits of being flexible.", "C) They enjoy doing engineering to the best degree.", "D) They have proved capable of doing almost anything."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题）\n【判型】What can we conclude about many female engineers from the statement of a\nmechanical engineer 中的 conclude+from 是典型推断题信号，需要从一位受访者的具体\n话语得出\"许多女工程师\"层面的结论。\n【拆句】What can we conclude about many female engineers from the statement of a\nmechanical engineer 主干为 What can we conclude，about many female engineers\n是 conclude 的对象，from the statement of a mechanical engineer 是信息来源；问的\n是\"从这位机械工程师的话里能得出关于许多女工程师的什么结论\"。\n【定位】Many women engineers hailed the benefits of their chosen career. A program manager in\nmanufacturing stated that engineering is the best degree. A mechanical engineer said, \"It will\ngive you the flexibility to do almost anything. It is also satisfying to see the effects of what\nyou have done.\"\n许多女工程师都盛赞自己所选职业的好处。一位制造业项目经理说工程是最好的学位。一\n位机械工程师说：\"它会给你几乎可以做任何事的灵活度，看到自己所做工作的成果也令人\n满足。\"\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nMany women engineers hailed the benefits of their chosen career ↔\nThey take great pride in their chosen career\nIt is also satisfying to see the effects of what you have done ↔\ntake great pride in their chosen career\n预判要\"对职业感到自豪\"，A 项 take great pride in their chosen career 在态度极性和\n概括层级上都对得最准，是最贴预判的一项。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 give you the flexibility to do almost anything 是说\"这份职业能给你灵活度\"，\n强调的是职业本身的属性；B 项 reaped the benefits of being flexible 把它说成\"她们\n因\"自己\"灵活而获益\"，把工具属性挪到了人本身。\nC) 无中生有\n原文 engineering is the best degree 是\"工程是最好的学位\"，整体作主系表；C 项\nenjoy doing engineering to the best degree 把 the best degree 拆成 to the best\ndegree（=最大程度地）作状语，意思变成\"以最高程度享受做工程\"，是字面拼凑出的\n伪意思。\nD) 过度推断\n原文 give you the flexibility to do almost anything 是工程这个职业\"能让你\"做几乎\n任何事，是潜在可能；D 项 proved capable of doing almost anything 说她们\"已经\n证明自己能做几乎任何事\"，把可能性升级为既定事实。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}]});
